--- Lory's data table engine: the row model a data grid stands on, as plain
--- functions over rows of cells and column definitions, with nothing drawn.
--- The drawing (headers, resizing, rows, cells) stands on it.
---
---     use lory.datatable as dt
---
---     columns = [dt.column['name', 'Name'], dt.column['age', 'Age']]
---     rows = [dt.row['p1', map[name = dt.words['Ada'], age = dt.number[36]]]]
---     t = dt.new[columns, rows]
---     t = dt.sort_toggled[t, 'age', false]
---     m = dt.models[t]
---     … m.page.rows are the rows to draw, in order …
---
--- It does what TanStack Table's table-core (9.3.1) does and answers what
--- it answers: sorting (one column or several, six sort functions, the
--- toggle cycle), filtering (column filters, a global filter, fuzzy ranks
--- as match-sorter-utils ranks them, sub-rows filtered from the root or from
--- the leaves, faceted values and ranges), grouping with aggregation,
--- expanding, pages, row selection, row pinning, and columns (visibility,
--- order, pinning, sizes, resizing, header groups). Grid.js's search, its
--- sort cycle and its pagination (the buttons and the words) are here too.
---
--- Every behaviour was written anew from the libraries' documentation and
--- from running them: tests/datatable/reference.mjs runs TanStack Table,
--- match-sorter-utils and Grid.js on fixed data and writes what they
--- answered to reference.json, and tests/datatable/reference.co holds this
--- module to it, value for value. No library's source, prose or data table
--- is copied; the accent fold the fuzzy ranks use is written here from
--- Unicode's letters.
---
--- A table is a record: its column definitions, its rows, its options and
--- its state. A function that changes the state answers the table changed
--- (`t = dt.sorted_by[t, [dt.Sort['age', true]]]`) and applies TanStack's
--- automatic resets there and then (a new filter goes back to the first
--- page), as a page that draws after each change sees them. `models[t]`
--- computes the rows the state makes, once; the functions that answer
--- about rows take them (`dt.page_count[t, m]`).
---
--- A cell is a Value, which says which JavaScript value TanStack would
--- have held there (undefined, null, a number, text, a truth, a date or a
--- list), because the libraries' answers turn on it: a missing age sorts
--- after the others, a null one counts as 0 to a range, text that holds
--- digits sorts as words and numbers. Two things differ, and say so where
--- they act: a date is the same as any date of its instant (JavaScript
--- compares two Date objects by identity), and a date's text is the one
--- JavaScript writes in UTC (where JavaScript writes the machine's local
--- time, so the reference is made in UTC).
use bits

--- What a cell holds: `is` names the kind ('missing', 'null', 'number',
--- 'text', 'bool', 'date' or 'list'); a number, a truth (1 or 0) and a date
--- (milliseconds since 1970) are held in `num`, text in `words` and a list's
--- items in `items`.
struct Value [is, num, words, items]

--- A row: its id, where it stands among its siblings (`index`), how deep it
--- is, the id of its parent ('' at the top), its cells by key and its
--- sub-rows; a group row also names the column it groups by (`column`, ''
--- for a row of data), the value it groups as text (`group`) and the ids of
--- the rows of data it holds (`leaves`). `ranks` holds the fuzzy rank each
--- column's filter gave the row.
struct Row [id, index, depth, parent, cells, subs, column, group, leaves, ranks]

--- A column definition. `key` is the cell a column reads ('' for a column
--- that only draws, which cannot sort, filter or group); `columns` holds a
--- group's children. The rest are TanStack's column options under plain
--- names (see `column`).
struct Column [id, header, key, columns, sort_with, first, absent, invert, sortable, multi, filter_with, filterable, searchable, aggregate_with, groupable, hideable, pinnable, size, smallest, largest, resizable]

--- The table's options: TanStack's under plain names (see `options`).
struct Options [sortable, multi, removable, multi_removable, most_sorted, first, manual_sorting, reset_sorting, filtering, column_filtering, searching, search_filter, from_leaves, filter_depth, manual_filtering, groupable, grouped_columns, manual_grouping, expandable, page_expanded, manual_expanding, reset_expanded, manual_pages, known_pages, known_rows, reset_pages, selectable, unselectable, multi_select, select_subs, closed, row_pinnable, unpinnable, keep_pinned, hideable, column_pinnable, resizable, resize_mode, resize_direction]

--- One column of the sorting: its id, and whether it sorts descending.
struct Sort [id, desc]

--- One column filter: the column's id and the value it filters by.
struct Filter [id, value]

--- A column's size when a resize began.
struct Sized [id, size]

--- A resize under way: where the pointer began (`offset`), the header's
--- size then, how far the pointer has moved (`moved`, in pixels) and that
--- as a share of the size, the column being resized ('' when none is) and
--- the sizes of the columns under the header when it began.
struct Resizing [offset, size, moved, share, column, sizes]

--- A table's state: what TanStack holds in its state slices. `expanded_all`
--- is TanStack's `expanded: true`; `expanded`, `selected`, `visible` and
--- `sizes` are maps by id; `start` and `end` are the columns pinned to each
--- side, `top` and `bottom` the rows pinned there; `search` is the global
--- filter's value (missing for none).
struct State [sorting, filters, search, grouping, expanded_all, expanded, page_index, page_size, selected, visible, order, start, end, top, bottom, sizes, resizing]

--- A table: its column definitions, its rows of data, its options, its
--- state and the state it began with (what an automatic reset of the
--- expanded rows goes back to).
struct Table [columns, rows, options, state, initial]

--- Rows as one of TanStack's row models holds them: the rows at the top
--- (each with its sub-rows) and every row of the model in its order.
struct Model [rows, flat]

--- Every row model of a table, as its state makes them: the data's
--- (`core`), the filtered, the grouped, the sorted, the expanded and the
--- page's. (`held` finds a row of the grouped model by id, `found` one of
--- the data's: positions in their `flat` lists.)
struct Models [core, filtered, grouped, sorted, expanded, page, held, found]

--- A fuzzy rank: how well a value matched (0 to 7, as match-sorter's
--- rankings run) and whether that passes the threshold.
struct Rank [rank, passed]

--- One faceted value and how many rows hold it.
struct Facet [value, count]

--- A faceted range: whether any row held a number, the least and the greatest.
struct Range [found, least, greatest]

--- A header: its id, the id of the column it heads, how many columns and
--- rows it spans, whether it only holds a place (and its place's id), its
--- depth and index in its group, its size, where it starts and the ids of
--- the headers under it.
struct Header [id, column, columns, rows, placeholder, place, depth, index, size, offset, subs]

--- A row of headers: its id, its depth and its headers.
struct HeaderGroup [id, depth, headers]

-------------------------------------------------------------------- values

fn _no_values[]
    one = [Value['missing', 0.0, '', []]]
    slice[one, 0, 0]

--- A cell with no value: JavaScript's undefined.
fn missing[]
    Value['missing', 0.0, '', _no_values[]]

--- A cell holding null.
fn nothing[]
    Value['null', 0.0, '', _no_values[]]

--- A cell holding a number.
fn number[n]
    Value['number', n + 0.0, '', _no_values[]]

--- A cell holding text.
fn words[t]
    Value['text', 0.0, t, _no_values[]]

--- A cell holding a truth.
fn truth[b]
    if b
        return Value['bool', 1.0, '', _no_values[]]
    Value['bool', 0.0, '', _no_values[]]

--- A cell holding a date, given in milliseconds since 1970 (UTC).
fn date[ms]
    Value['date', ms + 0.0, '', _no_values[]]

--- A cell holding a list of values.
fn listed[vs]
    Value['list', 0.0, '', vs]

fn _nan[]
    0.0 / 0.0

fn _infinity[]
    1.0 / 0.0

fn _is_nan[x]
    x ne x

fn _is_digit[c]
    c gte '0' and c lte '9'

fn _blank[v]
    v.is eq 'missing' or v.is eq 'null'

--- The text JavaScript's String() gives a value: 'undefined', 'null', a
--- number as JavaScript writes it, text as it is, 'true' or 'false', a list's
--- items joined by commas (null and undefined as nothing), and a date as
--- JavaScript writes it in UTC (where JavaScript writes its local time).
fn shown[v]
    if v.is eq 'missing'
        return 'undefined'
    if v.is eq 'null'
        return 'null'
    if v.is eq 'number'
        return number_text[v.num]
    if v.is eq 'text'
        return v.words
    if v.is eq 'bool'
        if v.num ne 0.0
            return 'true'
        return 'false'
    if v.is eq 'date'
        return _date_text[v.num]
    parts = for x in v.items
        _list_part[x]
    join[parts, ',']

fn _list_part[x]
    if _blank[x]
        return ''
    shown[x]

fn _two[n]
    if n lt 10
        return '0`n`'
    '`n`'

fn _year_text[y]
    digits = '`abs[y]`'
    while len[digits] lt 4
        digits = '0`digits`'
    if y lt 0
        return '-`digits`'
    digits

--- A date's text as JavaScript's String() writes it in UTC: its weekday,
--- month, day, year, time and zone ('Wed Jun 01 2022 00:00:00 GMT+0000
--- (Coordinated Universal Time)'), or 'Invalid Date'. The day is counted
--- from 1970 by the proleptic Gregorian calendar.
fn _date_text[ms]
    if _is_nan[ms] or ms eq _infinity[] or ms eq 0.0 - _infinity[]
        return 'Invalid Date'
    whole = int[floor[ms]]
    days = whole / 86400000
    if whole % 86400000 ne 0 and whole lt 0
        days = days - 1
    seconds = (whole - days * 86400000) / 1000
    z = days + 719468
    era = z / 146097
    if z % 146097 ne 0 and z lt 0
        era = era - 1
    of_era = z - era * 146097
    year_of_era = (of_era - of_era / 1460 + of_era / 36524 - of_era / 146096) / 365
    of_year = of_era - (365 * year_of_era + year_of_era / 4 - year_of_era / 100)
    shifted = (5 * of_year + 2) / 153
    day = of_year - (153 * shifted + 2) / 5 + 1
    month = shifted + 3
    if shifted gte 10
        month = shifted - 9
    year = year_of_era + era * 400
    if month lte 2
        year = year + 1
    weekday = ['Sun', 'Mon', 'Tue', 'Wed', 'Thu', 'Fri', 'Sat'][((days % 7) + 11) % 7]
    named = ['Jan', 'Feb', 'Mar', 'Apr', 'May', 'Jun', 'Jul', 'Aug', 'Sep', 'Oct', 'Nov', 'Dec'][month - 1]
    clock = '`_two[seconds / 3600]`:`_two[(seconds % 3600) / 60]`:`_two[seconds % 60]`'
    '`weekday` `named` `_two[day]` `_year_text[year]` `clock` GMT+0000 (Coordinated Universal Time)'

--- A number as JavaScript writes it: the fewest digits that read back as
--- it, positional from a millionth up to 10^21 and with an exponent beyond
--- (`1e-7`, `1e+21`), 'NaN', 'Infinity' and '-Infinity'.
fn number_text[x]
    if _is_nan[x]
        return 'NaN'
    if x eq _infinity[]
        return 'Infinity'
    if x eq 0.0 - _infinity[]
        return '-Infinity'
    if x eq 0.0
        return '0'
    if x lt 0.0
        rest = number_text[0.0 - x]
        return '-`rest`'
    parts = split[str[x], '.']
    whole = parts[0]
    decimals = ''
    if len[parts] gt 1
        decimals = parts[1]
    digits = concat[whole, decimals]
    i = 0
    while i lt len[digits] and digits[i] eq '0'
        i = i + 1
    n = len[whole] - i
    digits = slice[digits, i, len[digits]]
    j = len[digits]
    while j gt 0 and digits[j - 1] eq '0'
        j = j - 1
    digits = slice[digits, 0, j]
    k = len[digits]
    if k lte n and n lte 21
        zeros = repeat['0', n - k]
        return '`digits``zeros`'
    if 0 lt n and n lte 21
        head = slice[digits, 0, n]
        after = slice[digits, n, k]
        return '`head`.`after`'
    if -6 lt n and n lte 0
        zeros = repeat['0', 0 - n]
        return '0.`zeros``digits`'
    power = n - 1
    mark = '+'
    if power lt 0
        mark = '-'
    size = abs[power]
    if k eq 1
        return '`digits`e`mark``size`'
    lead = digits[0]
    rest = slice[digits, 1, k]
    '`lead`.`rest`e`mark``size`'

--- The length of the decimal number written at the start of text (a sign,
--- digits with a point, an exponent, or Infinity), 0 when none is.
fn _decimal_length[t]
    n = len[t]
    i = 0
    if i lt n and (t[i] eq '+' or t[i] eq '-')
        i = i + 1
    if slice[t, i, i + 8] eq 'Infinity'
        return i + 8
    before = 0
    while i lt n and _is_digit[t[i]]
        i = i + 1
        before = before + 1
    after = 0
    if i lt n and t[i] eq '.'
        j = i + 1
        while j lt n and _is_digit[t[j]]
            j = j + 1
            after = after + 1
        if before gt 0 or after gt 0
            i = j
    if before eq 0 and after eq 0
        return 0
    if i lt n and (t[i] eq 'e' or t[i] eq 'E')
        j = i + 1
        if j lt n and (t[j] eq '+' or t[j] eq '-')
            j = j + 1
        k = j
        while k lt n and _is_digit[t[k]]
            k = k + 1
        if k gt j
            i = k
    i

fn _decimal_value[t]
    if 'Infinity' in t
        if starts[t, '-']
            return 0.0 - _infinity[]
        return _infinity[]
    f = float[t]
    if not f.ok
        return _nan[]
    0.0 + f

fn _digit_value[c]
    if _is_digit[c]
        return code[c] - code['0']
    lowered = lower[c]
    if lowered gte 'a' and lowered lte 'z'
        return code[lowered] - code['a'] + 10
    -1

fn _radix_value[t, radix]
    if len[t] eq 0
        return _nan[]
    total = 0.0
    for c in split[t, '']
        d = _digit_value[c]
        if d lt 0 or d gte radix
            return _nan[]
        total = total * radix + d
    total

--- The number JavaScript's Number() reads in text: blank text is 0, a
--- decimal number (or Infinity) written whole is that number, 0x, 0o and
--- 0b numbers are read in their bases, and anything else is NaN.
fn text_number[t]
    s = trim[t]
    if s eq ''
        return 0.0
    head = lower[slice[s, 0, 2]]
    if head eq '0x'
        return _radix_value[slice[s, 2, len[s]], 16]
    if head eq '0o'
        return _radix_value[slice[s, 2, len[s]], 8]
    if head eq '0b'
        return _radix_value[slice[s, 2, len[s]], 2]
    if _decimal_length[s] ne len[s]
        return _nan[]
    _decimal_value[s]

--- The number JavaScript's Number() gives a value: undefined is NaN, null
--- 0, a truth 1 or 0, a date its milliseconds, text as text_number reads it
--- and a list as its text reads.
fn js_number[v]
    if v.is eq 'missing'
        return _nan[]
    if v.is eq 'null'
        return 0.0
    if v.is eq 'text'
        return text_number[v.words]
    if v.is eq 'list'
        return text_number[shown[v]]
    v.num

--- The number JavaScript's parseFloat() reads at the start of a value's text.
fn parsed_float[v]
    if v.is eq 'number'
        return v.num
    s = trim[shown[v]]
    n = _decimal_length[s]
    if n eq 0
        return _nan[]
    _decimal_value[slice[s, 0, n]]

--- Whether two values are the same as JavaScript's === finds them: of one
--- kind and equal, NaN never; a list is never the same as another (two
--- arrays are two objects), and a date is the same as a date of its instant.
fn strictly_same[a, b]
    if a.is ne b.is
        return false
    if _blank[a]
        return true
    if a.is eq 'text'
        return a.words eq b.words
    if a.is eq 'list'
        return false
    a.num eq b.num

--- Whether two values are the same as a JavaScript Map, a Set or includes()
--- finds them (SameValueZero): as strictly_same, except that NaN is NaN.
fn same_value[a, b]
    if a.is eq 'number' and b.is eq 'number' and _is_nan[a.num] and _is_nan[b.num]
        return true
    strictly_same[a, b]

--- Whether two values are equal as JavaScript's == finds them: null and
--- undefined equal each other only, a number equals text that reads as it,
--- a truth counts as 1 or 0, and a list compares as its text.
fn loosely_same[a, b]
    if a.is eq b.is
        return strictly_same[a, b]
    if _blank[a] or _blank[b]
        return _blank[a] and _blank[b]
    if a.is eq 'bool'
        return loosely_same[number[a.num], b]
    if b.is eq 'bool'
        return loosely_same[a, number[b.num]]
    if a.is eq 'number' and b.is eq 'text'
        return a.num eq text_number[b.words]
    if a.is eq 'text' and b.is eq 'number'
        return text_number[a.words] eq b.num
    if a.is eq 'list' and (b.is eq 'number' or b.is eq 'text')
        return loosely_same[words[shown[a]], b]
    if b.is eq 'list' and (a.is eq 'number' or a.is eq 'text')
        return loosely_same[a, words[shown[b]]]
    false

--- A value as JavaScript's < and > take it: a date as its milliseconds, a
--- list as its text, anything else as it is.
fn _primitive[v]
    if v.is eq 'date'
        return number[v.num]
    if v.is eq 'list'
        return words[shown[v]]
    v

--- Whether a is less than b as JavaScript's < finds it: text against text by
--- character codes, anything else as numbers (NaN is less than nothing, and
--- nothing is less than it).
fn js_less[a, b]
    pa = _primitive[a]
    pb = _primitive[b]
    if pa.is eq 'text' and pb.is eq 'text'
        return pa.words lt pb.words
    js_number[pa] lt js_number[pb]

-------------------------------------------------------------------- sort functions

--- The text TanStack's text sort functions read from a value: a finite
--- number as JavaScript writes it, text as it is, and nothing else ('').
fn _sort_text[v]
    if v.is eq 'number'
        if _is_nan[v.num] or v.num eq _infinity[] or v.num eq 0.0 - _infinity[]
            return ''
        return number_text[v.num]
    if v.is eq 'text'
        return v.words
    ''

fn _date_as_number[v]
    if v.is eq 'date'
        return number[v.num]
    v

--- How a sorts against b under one of TanStack's sort functions, below zero
--- when a comes first: 'alphanumeric' (words and the numbers in them, any
--- case), 'alphanumericCaseSensitive', 'text' (any case),
--- 'textCaseSensitive', 'datetime' (a date by its instant, anything else as
--- JavaScript's < and > take it) and 'basic' (JavaScript's === and >).
fn sort_compare[name, a, b]
    if name eq 'alphanumeric'
        return alphanumeric_compare[lower[_sort_text[a]], lower[_sort_text[b]]]
    if name eq 'alphanumericCaseSensitive'
        return alphanumeric_compare[_sort_text[a], _sort_text[b]]
    if name eq 'text'
        return _text_compare[lower[_sort_text[a]], lower[_sort_text[b]]]
    if name eq 'textCaseSensitive'
        return _text_compare[_sort_text[a], _sort_text[b]]
    if name eq 'datetime'
        pa = _date_as_number[a]
        pb = _date_as_number[b]
        if js_less[pb, pa]
            return 1
        if js_less[pa, pb]
            return -1
        return 0
    if strictly_same[a, b]
        return 0
    if js_less[b, a]
        return 1
    -1

fn _text_compare[a, b]
    if a eq b
        return 0
    if a gt b
        return 1
    -1

fn _chunk_end[s, from, numeric]
    i = from + 1
    while i lt len[s] and _is_digit[s[i]] eq numeric
        i = i + 1
    i

fn _chunks_left[s, from]
    n = 0
    i = from
    while i lt len[s]
        n = n + 1
        i = _chunk_end[s, i, _is_digit[s[i]]]
    n

fn _number_chunk_compare[a, from_a, to_a, b, from_b, to_b]
    i = from_a
    while i lt to_a and a[i] eq '0'
        i = i + 1
    j = from_b
    while j lt to_b and b[j] eq '0'
        j = j + 1
    if i eq to_a and j eq to_b
        return 0
    x = _decimal_value[slice[a, from_a, to_a]]
    y = _decimal_value[slice[b, from_b, to_b]]
    if x gt y
        return 1
    if y gt x
        return -1
    0

--- How a sorts against b as TanStack's alphanumeric functions find it: in
--- runs of digits and of other characters, words by character codes and
--- then by length, a number after a word, numbers by value, and then the
--- one with fewer runs left first.
fn alphanumeric_compare[a, b]
    i = 0
    j = 0
    while i lt len[a] and j lt len[b]
        numeric_a = _is_digit[a[i]]
        numeric_b = _is_digit[b[j]]
        end_a = _chunk_end[a, i, numeric_a]
        end_b = _chunk_end[b, j, numeric_b]
        if numeric_a ne numeric_b
            if numeric_a
                return 1
            return -1
        if numeric_a
            n = _number_chunk_compare[a, i, end_a, b, j, end_b]
            if n ne 0
                return n
        else
            w = _text_compare[slice[a, i, end_a], slice[b, j, end_b]]
            if w ne 0
                return w
        i = end_a
        j = end_b
    _chunks_left[a, i] - _chunks_left[b, j]

-------------------------------------------------------------------- filter functions

fn _falsy[v]
    _blank[v] or (v.is eq 'text' and v.words eq '')

fn _has_length[v]
    (v.is eq 'list' and len[v.items] gt 0) or (v.is eq 'text' and len[v.words] gt 0)

--- The value a filter function compares with, as TanStack resolves it once
--- per filter: the text functions read it as text (in lower case where case
--- is ignored), a range reads its two ends as numbers or dates (an end that
--- is null or reads as no number is open; ends the wrong way round are
--- swapped), and the others take it as it is.
fn resolved_filter[name, value]
    if name in ['includesString', 'equalsString', 'startsWith', 'endsWith']
        return words[lower[shown[value]]]
    if name in ['includesStringSensitive', 'equalsStringSensitive']
        return words[shown[value]]
    if name eq 'inNumberRange'
        return _number_range[value]
    if name eq 'inDateRange'
        return _date_range[value]
    value

fn _range_end[v, open]
    if v.is eq 'null'
        return open
    x = parsed_float[v]
    if _is_nan[x]
        return open
    x

fn _end_of[value, i]
    if value.is eq 'list' and len[value.items] gt i
        return value.items[i]
    if value.is eq 'text' and len[value.words] gt i
        return words[value.words[i]]
    missing[]

fn _ordered_range[low, high]
    if low gt high
        return listed[[number[high], number[low]]]
    listed[[number[low], number[high]]]

fn _number_range[value]
    low = _range_end[_end_of[value, 0], 0.0 - _infinity[]]
    high = _range_end[_end_of[value, 1], _infinity[]]
    _ordered_range[low, high]

--- A value as a date's milliseconds, as TanStack's date range reads it: a
--- date or a number as it is, a truth as 1 or 0, nothing (or blank text)
--- as NaN, and text as an ISO moment (JavaScript reads more ways of writing
--- a date).
fn _timestamp[v]
    if v.is eq 'date' or v.is eq 'number' or v.is eq 'bool'
        return v.num
    if v.is eq 'text' and v.words ne ''
        t = moment[v.words]
        if not t.ok
            return _nan[]
        return float[t] * 1000.0
    _nan[]

fn _date_range[value]
    low = _timestamp[_end_of[value, 0]]
    high = _timestamp[_end_of[value, 1]]
    if _is_nan[low]
        low = 0.0 - _infinity[]
    if _is_nan[high]
        high = _infinity[]
    _ordered_range[low, high]

fn _empty_cell[v]
    if _blank[v]
        return true
    trim[shown[v]] eq ''

fn _greater_than[data, filter]
    a = 0.0
    if not _blank[data]
        a = js_number[data]
    b = js_number[filter]
    if not _is_nan[a] and not _is_nan[b]
        return a gt b
    left = ''
    if not _blank[data]
        left = shown[data]
    trim[lower[left]] gt trim[lower[shown[filter]]]

fn _at_least[data, filter]
    strictly_same[data, filter] or _greater_than[data, filter]

fn _open_end[v]
    v.is eq 'missing' or (v.is eq 'text' and v.words eq '')

fn _between[data, bounds, inclusive]
    low = _end_of[bounds, 0]
    has_low = not _open_end[low]
    if has_low
        if inclusive and not _at_least[data, low]
            return false
        if not inclusive and not _greater_than[data, low]
            return false
    high = _end_of[bounds, 1]
    if _open_end[high]
        return true
    if has_low
        x = js_number[low]
        y = js_number[high]
        if not _is_nan[x] and not _is_nan[y] and x gt y
            return true
    if inclusive
        return not _greater_than[data, high]
    not _at_least[data, high]

fn _holds[list, v]
    for x in list.items
        if same_value[x, v]
            return true
    false

--- A filter value's items, as the list functions walk them: a list's
--- items, text's characters, and nothing for anything else.
fn _filter_items[filter]
    if filter.is eq 'list'
        return filter.items
    if filter.is eq 'text'
        out = for c in split[filter.words, '']
            words[c]
        return out
    _no_values[]

--- Whether a value passes one of TanStack's filter functions, given the
--- filter value as resolved_filter resolves it: 'includesString',
--- 'includesStringSensitive', 'equalsString', 'equalsStringSensitive',
--- 'startsWith', 'endsWith', 'equals', 'weakEquals', 'empty', 'notEmpty',
--- 'greaterThan', 'greaterThanOrEqualTo', 'lessThan', 'lessThanOrEqualTo',
--- 'between', 'betweenInclusive', 'inNumberRange', 'inDateRange', 'arrHas',
--- 'arrIncludes', 'arrIncludesAll' and 'arrIncludesSome'. ('fuzzy' ranks,
--- with rank_of.)
fn filter_passes[name, data, filter]
    if name in ['includesString', 'equalsString', 'startsWith', 'endsWith', 'includesStringSensitive', 'equalsStringSensitive']
        if _blank[data]
            return false
        held = shown[data]
        if name in ['includesString', 'equalsString', 'startsWith', 'endsWith']
            held = lower[held]
        if name in ['includesString', 'includesStringSensitive']
            return filter.words in held
        if name in ['equalsString', 'equalsStringSensitive']
            return held eq filter.words
        if name eq 'startsWith'
            return starts[held, filter.words]
        return ends[held, filter.words]
    if name eq 'equals'
        return strictly_same[data, filter]
    if name eq 'weakEquals'
        return loosely_same[data, filter]
    if name eq 'empty'
        return _empty_cell[data]
    if name eq 'notEmpty'
        return not _empty_cell[data]
    if name eq 'greaterThan'
        return _greater_than[data, filter]
    if name eq 'greaterThanOrEqualTo'
        return _at_least[data, filter]
    if name eq 'lessThan'
        return not _at_least[data, filter]
    if name eq 'lessThanOrEqualTo'
        return not _greater_than[data, filter]
    if name eq 'between'
        return _between[data, filter, false]
    if name eq 'betweenInclusive'
        return _between[data, filter, true]
    if name eq 'inNumberRange'
        if data.is ne 'number' or _is_nan[data.num]
            return false
        return data.num gte _end_of[filter, 0].num and data.num lte _end_of[filter, 1].num
    if name eq 'inDateRange'
        at = _timestamp[data]
        return at gte _end_of[filter, 0].num and at lte _end_of[filter, 1].num
    items = _filter_items[filter]
    if name eq 'arrHas'
        for x in items
            if strictly_same[data, x]
                return true
        return false
    if name eq 'arrIncludes'
        if data.is eq 'text'
            for x in items
                if shown[x] in data.words
                    return true
            return false
        if data.is ne 'list'
            return false
        for x in items
            if _holds[data, x]
                return true
        return false
    if data.is ne 'list'
        return false
    if name eq 'arrIncludesAll'
        for x in items
            if not _holds[data, x]
                return false
        return true
    if name eq 'arrIncludesSome'
        for x in items
            if _holds[data, x]
                return true
        return false
    false

--- Whether setting a filter to this value takes the filter away, as
--- TanStack's filter functions decide: undefined always does, null and ''
--- do for every built-in function, anything with no items does for the
--- list functions, a range with two empty ends does for the ranges, false
--- does for 'empty' and 'notEmpty', and a function with no rule of its own
--- ('fuzzy') takes away undefined and '' only.
fn filter_removes[name, value]
    if value.is eq 'missing'
        return true
    if name in ['arrHas', 'arrIncludes', 'arrIncludesAll', 'arrIncludesSome']
        return _falsy[value] or not _has_length[value]
    if name in ['between', 'betweenInclusive', 'inNumberRange', 'inDateRange']
        if _falsy[value]
            return true
        return value.is eq 'list' and _falsy[_end_of[value, 0]] and _falsy[_end_of[value, 1]]
    if name in ['empty', 'notEmpty']
        return _falsy[value] or (value.is eq 'bool' and value.num eq 0.0)
    if name in ['includesString', 'includesStringSensitive', 'equalsString', 'equalsStringSensitive', 'startsWith', 'endsWith', 'equals', 'weakEquals', 'greaterThan', 'greaterThanOrEqualTo', 'lessThan', 'lessThanOrEqualTo']
        return _falsy[value]
    value.is eq 'text' and value.words eq ''

-------------------------------------------------------------------- fuzzy ranks

--- A letter with its accent taken off (and Æ, Œ, Þ and their kind written
--- out), as the fuzzy ranks compare letters: Latin-1's and Latin
--- Extended-A's. Ł folds to a small l, as match-sorter's ranks fold it.
fn _plain_letter[c]
    if c lt 'À'
        return c
    if c in 'ÀÁÂÃÄÅĀĂĄ'
        return 'A'
    if c in 'àáâãäåāăą'
        return 'a'
    if c in 'ÇĆĈĊČ'
        return 'C'
    if c in 'çćĉċč'
        return 'c'
    if c in 'ÐĎĐ'
        return 'D'
    if c in 'ðďđ'
        return 'd'
    if c in 'ÈÉÊËĒĔĖĘĚ'
        return 'E'
    if c in 'èéêëēĕėęě'
        return 'e'
    if c in 'ĜĞĠĢ'
        return 'G'
    if c in 'ĝğġģ'
        return 'g'
    if c in 'ĤĦ'
        return 'H'
    if c in 'ĥħ'
        return 'h'
    if c in 'ÌÍÎÏĨĪĬĮİ'
        return 'I'
    if c in 'ìíîïĩīĭįı'
        return 'i'
    if c eq 'Ĵ'
        return 'J'
    if c eq 'ĵ'
        return 'j'
    if c eq 'Ķ'
        return 'K'
    if c eq 'ķ'
        return 'k'
    if c in 'ĹĻĽĿ'
        return 'L'
    if c eq 'Ł'
        return 'l'
    if c in 'ĺļľŀł'
        return 'l'
    if c in 'ÑŃŅŇ'
        return 'N'
    if c in 'ñńņňŉ'
        return 'n'
    if c in 'ÒÓÔÕÖØŌŎŐ'
        return 'O'
    if c in 'òóôõöøōŏő'
        return 'o'
    if c in 'ŔŖŘ'
        return 'R'
    if c in 'ŕŗř'
        return 'r'
    if c in 'ŚŜŞŠ'
        return 'S'
    if c in 'śŝşšſ'
        return 's'
    if c in 'ŢŤŦ'
        return 'T'
    if c in 'ţťŧ'
        return 't'
    if c in 'ÙÚÛÜŨŪŬŮŰŲ'
        return 'U'
    if c in 'ùúûüũūŭůűų'
        return 'u'
    if c eq 'Ŵ'
        return 'W'
    if c eq 'ŵ'
        return 'w'
    if c in 'ÝŶŸ'
        return 'Y'
    if c in 'ýÿŷ'
        return 'y'
    if c in 'ŹŻŽ'
        return 'Z'
    if c in 'źżž'
        return 'z'
    if c eq 'Æ'
        return 'AE'
    if c eq 'æ'
        return 'ae'
    if c eq 'Œ'
        return 'OE'
    if c eq 'œ'
        return 'oe'
    if c eq 'Þ'
        return 'TH'
    if c eq 'þ'
        return 'th'
    if c eq 'Ĳ'
        return 'IJ'
    if c eq 'ĳ'
        return 'ij'
    c

--- Text with its letters' accents taken off.
fn plain_letters[t]
    parts = for c in split[t, '']
        _plain_letter[c]
    join[parts, '']

fn _acronym[t]
    pieces = split[replace[t, '-', ' '], ' ']
    firsts = for piece in pieces
        slice[piece, 0, 1]
    join[firsts, '']

fn _found_from[t, c, from]
    j = from
    while j lt len[t]
        if t[j] eq c
            return j + 1
        j = j + 1
    -1

fn _closeness[t, q]
    first = _found_from[t, q[0], 0]
    if first lt 0
        return 0.0
    at = first
    k = 1
    while k lt len[q]
        at = _found_from[t, q[k], at]
        if at lt 0
            return 0.0
        k = k + 1
    1.0 + 1.0 / (at - first)

--- How well text matches a query, as match-sorter ranks it: 7 the same
--- text, 6 the same in any case, 5 starting with it, 4 a word starting with
--- it, 3 holding it, 2 its words' initials holding it, between 1 and 2 its
--- letters in order (closer letters rank higher), 0 no match. A query
--- longer than the text never matches, one letter matches only as a part,
--- and accents are taken off both first.
fn match_rank[item, query]
    t = plain_letters[item]
    q = plain_letters[query]
    if len[q] gt len[t]
        return 0.0
    if t eq q
        return 7.0
    t = lower[t]
    q = lower[q]
    if t eq q
        return 6.0
    if starts[t, q]
        return 5.0
    spaced = ' `q`'
    if spaced in t
        return 4.0
    if q in t
        return 3.0
    if len[q] eq 1
        return 0.0
    if q in _acronym[t]
        return 2.0
    _closeness[t, q]

--- A value's fuzzy rank against a query, as match-sorter's rankItem gives
--- it: the rank of the value's text, and whether it reaches the threshold
--- (1, letters in order, unless told).
fn rank_of[v, query, threshold = 1.0]
    r = match_rank[shown[v], shown[query]]
    Rank[r, r gte threshold]

--- How two fuzzy ranks sort, the better first (match-sorter's compareItems).
fn rank_compare[a, b]
    if a eq b
        return 0
    if a gt b
        return -1
    1

-------------------------------------------------------------------- columns

fn _no_columns[]
    one = [Column['', '', '', [], 'auto', 'auto', 'greater', false, true, 'auto', 'auto', true, 'auto', 'auto', true, true, true, 150.0, 20.0, 9007199254740991.0, true]]
    slice[one, 0, 0]

fn _no_words[]
    slice[[''], 0, 0]

fn _no_flags[]
    m = map['' = true]
    del[m, '']

fn _no_sizes[]
    m = map['' = 0.0]
    del[m, '']

--- A column that reads the cell under `key`, which is its id too. Its
--- options are TanStack's column options under plain names: `header`, its
--- words; `sort_with`, its sort function ('auto' chooses one from the first
--- rows' values, or one of sort_compare's names, or 'rank' for the fuzzy
--- rank its filter gave and then 'alphanumeric'); `first`, the direction
--- its first press sorts it ('auto': ascending for text, descending for
--- anything else; 'asc' or 'desc'); `absent`, where rows missing its value
--- sort ('greater': after the others ascending, as if larger; 'smaller';
--- 'first' and 'last' whichever the direction; 'compared': by the sort
--- function like any value); `invert`, whether its directions are turned
--- round (a ranking, where 1 is best); `multi`, whether it may join a sort
--- of several columns ('auto' as the table says, 'yes' or 'no'); `filter_with`,
--- its filter function ('auto' chooses one from the first value, or one of
--- filter_passes's names, or 'fuzzy'); `searchable`, whether the global
--- filter reads it ('auto': when the first value it holds is text or a
--- number; 'yes' or 'no'); `aggregate_with`, how a group row sums it up ('auto':
--- a sum of numbers, the extent of dates; 'none', or one of aggregated's
--- names); `size`, `smallest` and `largest`, its width in pixels and its
--- bounds; and whether it may be sorted, filtered, grouped, hidden, pinned
--- and resized.
fn column[key, header = '', sort_with = 'auto', first = 'auto', absent = 'greater', invert = false, sortable = true, multi = 'auto', filter_with = 'auto', filterable = true, searchable = 'auto', aggregate_with = 'auto', groupable = true, hideable = true, pinnable = true, size = 150.0, smallest = 20.0, largest = 9007199254740991.0, resizable = true]
    Column[key, header, key, _no_columns[], sort_with, first, absent, invert, sortable, multi, filter_with, filterable, searchable, aggregate_with, groupable, hideable, pinnable, size + 0.0, smallest + 0.0, largest + 0.0, resizable]

--- A column that reads no cell, for what a row draws beside its data (a
--- checkbox, a button): it cannot sort, filter, search or group, and a
--- group row sums nothing up in it.
fn display_column[id, header = '', hideable = true, pinnable = true, size = 150.0, smallest = 20.0, largest = 9007199254740991.0, resizable = true]
    Column[id, header, '', _no_columns[], 'auto', 'auto', 'greater', false, true, 'auto', 'auto', true, 'auto', 'none', true, hideable, pinnable, size + 0.0, smallest + 0.0, largest + 0.0, resizable]

--- A column over columns: a header that spans its children's.
fn column_group[id, header, columns, hideable = true, pinnable = true, size = 150.0, smallest = 20.0, largest = 9007199254740991.0, resizable = true]
    Column[id, header, '', columns, 'auto', 'auto', 'greater', false, true, 'auto', 'auto', true, 'auto', 'none', true, hideable, pinnable, size + 0.0, smallest + 0.0, largest + 0.0, resizable]

fn _nameless[]
    Column['', '', '', _no_columns[], 'auto', 'auto', 'greater', false, false, 'no', 'auto', false, 'no', 'none', false, false, false, 150.0, 20.0, 9007199254740991.0, false]

fn _flat_of[columns]
    out = _no_columns[]
    for c in columns
        out = push[out, c]
        if len[c.columns] gt 0
            out = concat[out, _flat_of[c.columns]]
    out

fn _leaves_of[columns]
    out = _no_columns[]
    for c in columns
        if len[c.columns] gt 0
            out = concat[out, _leaves_of[c.columns]]
        else
            out = push[out, c]
    out

fn _column_index[columns, id]
    i = 0
    while i lt len[columns]
        if columns[i].id eq id
            return i
        i = i + 1
    -1

fn _word_index[ws, w]
    i = 0
    while i lt len[ws]
        if ws[i] eq w
            return i
        i = i + 1
    -1

fn _without[ws, gone]
    out = _no_words[]
    for w in ws
        if not (w in gone)
            out = push[out, w]
    out

--- Columns in the order the table's state puts them: the ids the column
--- order names first (an id that is no column is passed over), then the
--- rest as they came; and then the grouped columns moved to the front in
--- the grouping's order ('reorder'), taken out ('remove'), or left where
--- they stand ('keep').
fn _ordered[t, columns]
    out = columns
    order = t.state.order
    if len[order] gt 0
        taken = _no_columns[]
        rest = columns
        i = 0
        while i lt len[order]
            k = _column_index[rest, order[i]]
            if k gte 0
                taken = push[taken, rest[k]]
                rest = concat[slice[rest, 0, k], slice[rest, k + 1, len[rest]]]
            i = i + 1
        out = concat[taken, rest]
    grouping = t.state.grouping
    mode = t.options.grouped_columns
    if len[grouping] eq 0 or mode eq 'keep'
        return out
    rest = _no_columns[]
    for c in out
        if not (c.id in grouping)
            rest = push[rest, c]
    if mode eq 'remove'
        return rest
    front = _no_columns[]
    for id in grouping
        k = _column_index[out, id]
        if k gte 0
            front = push[front, out[k]]
    concat[front, rest]

--- The table with its columns in this order: the ids it names first (an id
--- that is no column is passed over), then the rest as they were defined.
fn order_set[t, ids]
    t.state.order = ids
    t

--- The table's leaf columns in their order (the column order's, then the
--- grouping's), shown or not.
fn leaf_columns[t]
    _ordered[t, _leaves_of[t.columns]]

--- Every column, groups and leaves, as defined: each group before its children.
fn flat_columns[t]
    _flat_of[t.columns]

--- The column of an id, a group's too; a column with no id when the table has none.
fn column_of[t, id]
    flat = _flat_of[t.columns]
    k = _column_index[flat, id]
    if k lt 0
        return _nameless[]
    flat[k]

--- Whether the table has a column of this id.
fn has_column[t, id]
    _column_index[_flat_of[t.columns], id] gte 0

--- A column's leaves in their order: a leaf's are itself.
fn _column_leaves[t, c]
    if len[c.columns] eq 0
        return [c]
    _ordered[t, _leaves_of[c.columns]]

-------------------------------------------------------------------- visibility

fn _shows[t, c]
    if len[c.columns] eq 0
        if c.id in t.state.visible
            return t.state.visible[c.id]
        return true
    for d in c.columns
        if _shows[t, d]
            return true
    false

--- Whether a column shows: a leaf unless the state hides it, a group while
--- any of its children shows.
fn is_visible[t, id]
    _shows[t, column_of[t, id]]

--- The leaf columns that show, in the columns' order (not the pinning's).
fn visible_columns[t]
    out = _no_columns[]
    for c in leaf_columns[t]
        if _shows[t, c]
            out = push[out, c]
    out

--- Every column that shows, groups and leaves, as defined.
fn visible_flat_columns[t]
    out = _no_columns[]
    for c in _flat_of[t.columns]
        if _shows[t, c]
            out = push[out, c]
    out

fn _hideable[t, c]
    c.hideable and t.options.hideable

--- Whether a column may be hidden: it says so and the table does.
fn can_hide[t, id]
    _hideable[t, column_of[t, id]]

--- The table with a column shown or hidden; for a group, each of its leaves
--- that may hide. A column that may not hide is left as it is.
fn visibility_set[t, id, visible]
    c = column_of[t, id]
    if not _hideable[t, c]
        return t
    seen = t.state.visible
    for leaf in _column_leaves[t, c]
        if _hideable[t, leaf]
            seen = set[seen, leaf.id, visible]
    t.state.visible = seen
    t

--- The table with a column hidden if it showed and shown if it was hidden.
fn visibility_toggled[t, id]
    visibility_set[t, id, not is_visible[t, id]]

--- The table with every leaf column shown, or every one hidden that may hide.
fn all_visibility_set[t, visible]
    seen = _no_flags[]
    for c in leaf_columns[t]
        seen = set[seen, c.id, visible or not _hideable[t, c]]
    t.state.visible = seen
    t

--- The table with every column shown unless all showed, then hidden.
fn all_visibility_toggled[t]
    all_visibility_set[t, not all_visible[t]]

--- Whether every leaf column shows.
fn all_visible[t]
    for c in leaf_columns[t]
        if not _shows[t, c]
            return false
    true

--- Whether any leaf column shows.
fn some_visible[t]
    for c in leaf_columns[t]
        if _shows[t, c]
            return true
    false

-------------------------------------------------------------------- column pinning

--- The table with a column pinned to a side ('start' or 'end'), or freed
--- (''): a group's leaves go together, after what that side held.
fn column_pinned[t, id, side]
    ids = _no_words[]
    for leaf in _column_leaves[t, column_of[t, id]]
        if leaf.id ne ''
            ids = push[ids, leaf.id]
    leading = _without[t.state.start, ids]
    trailing = _without[t.state.end, ids]
    if side eq 'start'
        leading = concat[leading, ids]
    if side eq 'end'
        trailing = concat[trailing, ids]
    t.state.start = leading
    t.state.end = trailing
    t

--- Whether a column may be pinned: one of its leaves may, and the table lets columns pin.
fn can_pin_column[t, id]
    for leaf in _column_leaves[t, column_of[t, id]]
        if leaf.pinnable and t.options.column_pinnable
            return true
    false

--- The side a column is pinned to: 'start' when one of its leaves is
--- pinned there, else 'end' when one is pinned there, else ''.
fn column_pin_of[t, id]
    leaves = _column_leaves[t, column_of[t, id]]
    for leaf in leaves
        if leaf.id in t.state.start
            return 'start'
    for leaf in leaves
        if leaf.id in t.state.end
            return 'end'
    ''

--- Where a pinned column stands among its side's ids: 0 for a column not
--- pinned, -1 for a group (whose own id is never pinned, only its leaves').
fn pinned_column_index[t, id]
    side = column_pin_of[t, id]
    if side eq ''
        return 0
    if side eq 'start'
        return _word_index[t.state.start, id]
    _word_index[t.state.end, id]

--- Whether any column is pinned: to a side ('start' or 'end'), or to either ('').
fn some_columns_pinned[t, side = '']
    if side eq 'start'
        return len[t.state.start] gt 0
    if side eq 'end'
        return len[t.state.end] gt 0
    len[t.state.start] gt 0 or len[t.state.end] gt 0

fn _by_ids[columns, ids]
    out = _no_columns[]
    for id in ids
        k = _column_index[columns, id]
        if k gte 0
            out = push[out, columns[k]]
    out

--- The leaf columns that show in a part of the table: 'start' and 'end'
--- hold the columns pinned there in the order they were pinned, 'center'
--- the rest in the columns' order, and 'all' every one that shows, in the
--- columns' order.
fn side_columns[t, side]
    leaves = leaf_columns[t]
    out = _no_columns[]
    if side eq 'start' or side eq 'end'
        ids = t.state.start
        if side eq 'end'
            ids = t.state.end
        for c in _by_ids[leaves, ids]
            if _shows[t, c]
                out = push[out, c]
        return out
    pinned = concat[t.state.start, t.state.end]
    for c in leaves
        if _shows[t, c] and (side eq 'all' or not (c.id in pinned))
            out = push[out, c]
    out

--- The leaf columns that show in the order a table draws them: the start's
--- pinned columns, the center's, then the end's.
fn drawn_columns[t]
    concat[side_columns[t, 'start'], side_columns[t, 'center'], side_columns[t, 'end']]

-------------------------------------------------------------------- column sizes

fn _size_of[t, c]
    size = c.size
    if c.id in t.state.sizes
        size = t.state.sizes[c.id]
    min[max[c.smallest, size], c.largest]

--- A column's width in pixels: its size in the state, or its own, kept
--- within its smallest and largest.
fn column_size[t, id]
    _size_of[t, column_of[t, id]]

--- How far into a part of the table ('all', 'start', 'center' or 'end') a
--- column begins: the widths of the columns before it, 0 for a column not
--- there.
fn column_start[t, id, side = 'all']
    at = 0.0
    for c in side_columns[t, side]
        if c.id eq id
            return at
        at = at + _size_of[t, c]
    0.0

--- The widths of the columns after a column in a part of the table, 0 for
--- a column not there.
fn column_after[t, id, side = 'all']
    columns = side_columns[t, side]
    after = 0.0
    i = len[columns] - 1
    while i gte 0
        if columns[i].id eq id
            return after
        after = after + _size_of[t, columns[i]]
        i = i - 1
    0.0

--- Where a column stands among the columns that show in a part of the
--- table, -1 when it is not there.
fn column_index[t, id, side = 'all']
    _column_index[side_columns[t, side], id]

--- Whether a column is the first that shows in a part of the table.
fn is_first_column[t, id, side = 'all']
    columns = side_columns[t, side]
    len[columns] gt 0 and columns[0].id eq id

--- Whether a column is the last that shows in a part of the table.
fn is_last_column[t, id, side = 'all']
    columns = side_columns[t, side]
    len[columns] gt 0 and columns[len[columns] - 1].id eq id

--- The width of every column that shows: the top row of headers' widths, together.
fn total_size[t]
    groups = header_groups[t, 'all']
    total = 0.0
    if len[groups] eq 0
        return total
    for h in groups[0].headers
        total = total + h.size
    total

--- The width of the columns that show in one part of the table ('start',
--- 'center' or 'end').
fn side_size[t, side]
    total = 0.0
    for c in side_columns[t, side]
        total = total + _size_of[t, c]
    total

--- The table with a column's width set (its bounds still hold it).
fn column_size_set[t, id, size]
    t.state.sizes = set[t.state.sizes, id, size + 0.0]
    t

--- The table with a column's width back to its own.
fn column_size_reset[t, id]
    if id in t.state.sizes
        t.state.sizes = del[t.state.sizes, id]
    t

--- Whether a column may be resized: it says so and the table does.
fn can_resize[t, id]
    c = column_of[t, id]
    c.resizable and t.options.resizable

--- Whether a resize is under way on this column.
fn is_resizing[t, id]
    t.state.resizing.column ne '' and t.state.resizing.column eq id

fn _depth_in[columns, id, depth]
    for c in columns
        if c.id eq id
            return depth
        if len[c.columns] gt 0
            d = _depth_in[c.columns, id, depth + 1]
            if d gte 0
                return d
    -1

fn _parent_in[columns, id, parent]
    for c in columns
        if c.id eq id
            return parent
        if len[c.columns] gt 0
            p = _parent_in[c.columns, id, c.id]
            if p ne '-'
                return p
    '-'

--- How deep a column stands among the groups: 0 at the top.
fn column_depth[t, id]
    _depth_in[t.columns, id, 0]

--- The id of the group a column stands in, '' at the top.
fn column_parent[t, id]
    p = _parent_in[t.columns, id, '']
    if p eq '-'
        return ''
    p

-------------------------------------------------------------------- header groups

fn _no_headers[]
    one = [Header['', '', 0, 0, false, '', 0, 0, 0.0, 0.0, _no_words[]]]
    slice[one, 0, 0]

fn _no_groups[]
    one = [HeaderGroup['', 0, _no_headers[]]]
    slice[one, 0, 0]

fn _no_heads[]
    m = map['' = Header['', '', 0, 0, false, '', 0, 0, 0.0, 0.0, _no_words[]]]
    del[m, '']

fn _header_depth[t, columns, depth]
    deepest = depth
    for c in columns
        if len[c.columns] gt 0 and _shows[t, c]
            deepest = max[deepest, _header_depth[t, c.columns, depth + 1]]
    deepest

fn _depths_into[m, columns, depth]
    out = m
    for c in columns
        out = set[out, c.id, depth]
        if len[c.columns] gt 0
            out = _depths_into[out, c.columns, depth + 1]
    out

fn _parents_into[m, columns, parent]
    out = m
    for c in columns
        out = set[out, c.id, parent]
        if len[c.columns] gt 0
            out = _parents_into[out, c.columns, c.id]
    out

fn _joined_id[id, part]
    if part eq ''
        return id
    if id eq ''
        return part
    '`id`_`part`'

fn _header_id[family, depth, owner, child]
    id = family
    if depth ne 0
        id = _joined_id[id, str[depth]]
    id = _joined_id[id, owner]
    _joined_id[id, child]

fn _group_id[family, depth]
    if family eq ''
        return str[depth]
    '`family`_`depth`'

fn _spanned[heads, ids]
    out = heads
    for id in ids
        h = out[id]
        if len[h.subs] gt 0
            out = _spanned[out, h.subs]
            span = 0
            for s in h.subs
                span = span + out[s].columns
            h.columns = span
        else
            h.columns = 1
        h.rows = 1
        if h.placeholder and len[h.subs] eq 1 and out[h.subs[0]].column eq h.column
            spans = 1
            chain = h.subs[0]
            while chain ne ''
                c = out[chain]
                c.rows = 0
                out = set[out, chain, c]
                spans = spans + 1
                chain = ''
                if len[c.subs] eq 1 and out[c.subs[0]].column eq h.column
                    chain = c.subs[0]
            h.rows = spans
        out = set[out, id, h]
    out

fn _header_size[t, heads, id]
    h = heads[id]
    if len[h.subs] eq 0
        return _size_of[t, column_of[t, h.column]]
    total = 0.0
    for s in h.subs
        total = total + _header_size[t, heads, s]
    total

--- The rows of headers over a part of the table ('all', 'start', 'center'
--- or 'end'), the top row first, as TanStack's getHeaderGroups lays them
--- out: every row of headers reaches down to the deepest group that shows;
--- a header over a group spans its leaves' columns; where a column has no
--- group above it, a placeholder header of that column stands in for one,
--- and the topmost of a column's placeholders spans the rows down to its
--- own header, the ones under it spanning none. Each header has its id
--- (its depth, its column and the id of the header under it, joined), its
--- width and where it starts in its row.
fn header_groups[t, side]
    leaves = drawn_columns[t]
    family = ''
    if side ne 'all'
        leaves = side_columns[t, side]
        family = side
    deepest = _header_depth[t, t.columns, 1]
    depths = _depths_into[map['' = 0], t.columns, 0]
    parents = _parents_into[map['' = ''], t.columns, '']
    level = _no_headers[]
    i = 0
    while i lt len[leaves]
        level = push[level, Header[leaves[i].id, leaves[i].id, 0, 0, false, '', deepest, i, 0.0, 0.0, _no_words[]]]
        i = i + 1
    built = _no_groups[]
    depth = deepest - 1
    going = true
    while going
        pending = _no_headers[]
        for h in level
            above = h.column
            placeholder = true
            if depths[h.column] eq depth and parents[h.column] ne ''
                above = parents[h.column]
                placeholder = false
            last = len[pending] - 1
            if last gte 0 and pending[last].column eq above
                held = pending[last]
                held.subs = push[held.subs, h.id]
                pending[last] = held
            else
                place = ''
                if placeholder
                    count = 0
                    for p in pending
                        if p.column eq above
                            count = count + 1
                    place = str[count]
                subs = push[_no_words[], h.id]
                pending = push[pending, Header[_header_id[family, depth, above, h.id], above, 0, 0, placeholder, place, depth, len[pending], 0.0, 0.0, subs]]
        built = push[built, HeaderGroup[_group_id[family, depth], depth, level]]
        if depth gt 0
            level = pending
            depth = depth - 1
        else
            going = false
    heads = _no_heads[]
    for g in built
        for h in g.headers
            heads = set[heads, h.id, h]
    top = _no_words[]
    if len[built] gt 0
        for h in built[len[built] - 1].headers
            top = push[top, h.id]
    heads = _spanned[heads, top]
    out = _no_groups[]
    k = len[built] - 1
    while k gte 0
        g = built[k]
        level = _no_headers[]
        at = 0.0
        for h in g.headers
            done = heads[h.id]
            done.size = _header_size[t, heads, h.id]
            done.offset = at
            at = at + done.size
            level = push[level, done]
        out = push[out, HeaderGroup[g.id, g.depth, level]]
        k = k - 1
    out

--- Every header of the table's rows of headers, the top row's first.
fn flat_headers[t]
    out = _no_headers[]
    for g in header_groups[t, 'all']
        out = concat[out, g.headers]
    out

-------------------------------------------------------------------- resizing

fn _no_sized[]
    one = [Sized['', 0.0]]
    slice[one, 0, 0]

--- No resize under way.
fn _still[]
    Resizing[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, '', _no_sized[]]

fn _under[heads, id]
    out = _no_headers[]
    for s in heads[id].subs
        out = concat[out, _under[heads, s]]
    push[out, heads[id]]

--- The table with a resize begun on a header (its id, as header_groups
--- gives it) by a pointer at `x`, when its column may resize: the widths
--- of the columns under the header (and of its own) are kept as they stand,
--- each to grow by the same share as the header.
fn resize_started[t, header_id, x]
    heads = _no_heads[]
    for h in flat_headers[t]
        heads = set[heads, h.id, h]
    if not (header_id in heads)
        return t
    h = heads[header_id]
    if not can_resize[t, h.column]
        return t
    sizes = _no_sized[]
    for under in _under[heads, header_id]
        sizes = push[sizes, Sized[under.column, column_size[t, under.column]]]
    t.state.resizing = Resizing[x + 0.0, h.size, 0.0, 0.0, h.column, sizes]
    t

fn _resized[t, x, ended]
    r = t.state.resizing
    if r.column eq ''
        return t
    turn = 1.0
    if t.options.resize_direction eq 'rtl'
        turn = -1.0
    moved = (x - r.offset) * turn
    share = 0.0
    if r.size gt 0.0
        share = moved / r.size
    share = max[share, -0.999999]
    r.moved = moved
    r.share = share
    t.state.resizing = r
    if t.options.resize_mode eq 'change' or ended
        sizes = t.state.sizes
        for s in r.sizes
            size = moved / len[r.sizes]
            if s.size gt 0.0
                size = s.size + s.size * share
            sizes = set[sizes, s.id, round[max[size, 0.0] * 100.0] / 100.0]
        t.state.sizes = sizes
    t

--- The table with the pointer of a resize moved to `x`: the share it moved
--- is kept, and the widths change as it moves when the table resizes on
--- change (`resize_mode` 'change'), not only at the end ('end').
fn resize_moved[t, x]
    _resized[t, x + 0.0, false]

--- The table with a resize ended at `x`: the widths take the share moved.
fn resize_ended[t, x]
    if t.state.resizing.column eq ''
        return t
    t = _resized[t, x + 0.0, true]
    t.state.resizing = _still[]
    t

-------------------------------------------------------------------- rows

fn _no_cells[]
    m = map['' = missing[]]
    del[m, '']

fn _no_ranks[]
    m = map['' = 0.0]
    del[m, '']

fn _no_rows[]
    one = [Row['', 0, 0, '', _no_cells[], [], '', '', _no_words[], _no_ranks[]]]
    slice[one, 0, 0]

--- A row of data: its id and its cells by key.
fn row[id, cells]
    Row[id, 0, 0, '', cells, _no_rows[], '', '', _no_words[], _no_ranks[]]

--- A row of data with rows under it (a tree's), each a row of its own.
fn tree_row[id, cells, subs]
    Row[id, 0, 0, '', cells, subs, '', '', _no_words[], _no_ranks[]]

fn _unfound[]
    Row['', -1, 0, '', _no_cells[], _no_rows[], '', '', _no_words[], _no_ranks[]]

--- What a row holds in a column: its cell under the column's key, missing
--- when it has none (and always for a column that reads no cell).
fn value_of[r, c]
    if c.key eq '' or not (c.key in r.cells)
        return missing[]
    r.cells[c.key]

--- Whether a row stands for a group of rows.
fn is_group[r]
    r.column ne ''

fn _flattened[rows]
    out = _no_rows[]
    for r in rows
        out = push[out, r]
        if len[r.subs] gt 0
            out = concat[out, _flattened[r.subs]]
    out

fn _positions[flat]
    m = map['' = 0]
    m = del[m, '']
    i = 0
    while i lt len[flat]
        m = set[m, flat[i].id, i]
        i = i + 1
    m

fn _cored[rows, depth, parent]
    out = _no_rows[]
    i = 0
    while i lt len[rows]
        r = rows[i]
        r.index = i
        r.depth = depth
        r.parent = parent
        r.subs = _cored[r.subs, depth + 1, r.id]
        out = push[out, r]
        i = i + 1
    out

--- The data's rows as TanStack's core row model holds them: each knows its
--- place among its siblings, its depth and its parent.
fn core_model[t]
    rows = _cored[t.rows, 0, '']
    Model[rows, _flattened[rows]]

-------------------------------------------------------------------- filtering rows

--- What the filters made of each row of data: the ids of the filters that
--- judge (each column filter's, and '__global__' for the global filter),
--- each row's verdicts by filter id, and the fuzzy ranks its columns got.
struct Fates [ids, flags, ranks]

fn _search_on[s]
    not _falsy[s.search]

fn _filters_on[s]
    len[s.filters] gt 0 or _search_on[s]

fn _filter_names[]
    ['includesString', 'includesStringSensitive', 'equalsString', 'equalsStringSensitive', 'startsWith', 'endsWith', 'equals', 'weakEquals', 'empty', 'notEmpty', 'greaterThan', 'greaterThanOrEqualTo', 'lessThan', 'lessThanOrEqualTo', 'between', 'betweenInclusive', 'inNumberRange', 'inDateRange', 'arrHas', 'arrIncludes', 'arrIncludesAll', 'arrIncludesSome', 'fuzzy']

--- The filter function a column filters with, '' for none: its own, or
--- for 'auto' one chosen by the first value the data's rows hold in it
--- (text 'includesString', a number 'inNumberRange', a truth 'equals', a
--- list 'arrIncludes', a date 'inDateRange', and otherwise 'weakEquals').
fn _filter_name[c, core]
    if c.filter_with ne 'auto'
        if c.filter_with in _filter_names[]
            return c.filter_with
        return ''
    for r in core.flat
        v = value_of[r, c]
        if not _blank[v]
            if v.is eq 'text'
                return 'includesString'
            if v.is eq 'number'
                return 'inNumberRange'
            if v.is eq 'bool'
                return 'equals'
            if v.is eq 'list'
                return 'arrIncludes'
            return 'inDateRange'
    'weakEquals'

--- The filter function the global filter searches with, '' for none.
fn _search_name[t]
    name = t.options.search_filter
    if name eq 'auto'
        return 'includesString'
    if name in _filter_names[]
        return name
    ''

--- Whether the global filter reads a column: the column and the table let
--- it, the column reads a cell, and (unless the column says 'yes') the
--- first value the data's rows hold in it is text or a number.
fn _searchable[t, c, core]
    if c.searchable eq 'no' or not t.options.searching or not t.options.filtering or c.key eq ''
        return false
    if c.searchable eq 'yes'
        return true
    for r in core.flat
        v = value_of[r, c]
        if not _blank[v]
            return v.is eq 'text' or v.is eq 'number'
    false

--- The leaf columns the global filter reads, in their order.
fn search_columns[t, core]
    out = _no_columns[]
    for c in leaf_columns[t]
        if _searchable[t, c, core]
            out = push[out, c]
    out

fn _judging[name, v, resolved]
    if name eq 'fuzzy'
        return rank_of[v, resolved].passed
    filter_passes[name, v, resolved]

--- What the table's filters make of each row of data.
fn fates_of[t, core]
    s = t.state
    ids = _no_words[]
    names = _no_words[]
    judged = _no_columns[]
    wanted = _no_values[]
    for f in s.filters
        ids = push[ids, f.id]
        c = column_of[t, f.id]
        if c.id ne ''
            name = _filter_name[c, core]
            if name ne ''
                names = push[names, name]
                judged = push[judged, c]
                if name eq 'fuzzy'
                    wanted = push[wanted, f.value]
                else
                    wanted = push[wanted, resolved_filter[name, f.value]]
    searching = _no_columns[]
    search_by = ''
    sought = missing[]
    if _search_on[s]
        search_by = _search_name[t]
        if search_by ne ''
            searching = search_columns[t, core]
        if len[searching] gt 0
            ids = push[ids, '__global__']
            sought = s.search
            if search_by ne 'fuzzy'
                sought = resolved_filter[search_by, s.search]
    flags = map['' = _no_flags[]]
    flags = del[flags, '']
    ranks = map['' = _no_ranks[]]
    ranks = del[ranks, '']
    for r in core.flat
        verdicts = _no_flags[]
        got = _no_ranks[]
        j = 0
        while j lt len[judged]
            v = value_of[r, judged[j]]
            if names[j] eq 'fuzzy'
                rk = rank_of[v, wanted[j]]
                got = set[got, judged[j].id, rk.rank]
                verdicts = set[verdicts, judged[j].id, rk.passed]
            else
                verdicts = set[verdicts, judged[j].id, filter_passes[names[j], v, wanted[j]]]
            j = j + 1
        if len[searching] gt 0
            found = false
            k = 0
            while k lt len[searching] and not found
                v = value_of[r, searching[k]]
                if search_by eq 'fuzzy'
                    rk = rank_of[v, sought]
                    got = set[got, searching[k].id, rk.rank]
                    found = rk.passed
                else
                    found = filter_passes[search_by, v, sought]
                k = k + 1
            verdicts = set[verdicts, '__global__', found]
        flags = set[flags, r.id, verdicts]
        ranks = set[ranks, r.id, got]
    Fates[ids, flags, ranks]

fn _passes[fates, id, ids]
    verdicts = fates.flags[id]
    for f in ids
        if f in verdicts and not verdicts[f]
            return false
    true

fn _ranked[rows, fates]
    out = _no_rows[]
    for r in rows
        if r.id in fates.ranks
            r.ranks = fates.ranks[r.id]
        if len[r.subs] gt 0
            r.subs = _ranked[r.subs, fates]
        out = push[out, r]
    out

fn _from_root[rows, fates, ids, depth, most]
    out = _no_rows[]
    for r in rows
        if _passes[fates, r.id, ids]
            if len[r.subs] gt 0 and depth lt most
                r.subs = _from_root[r.subs, fates, ids, depth + 1, most]
            out = push[out, r]
    out

fn _from_leaves[rows, fates, ids, depth, most]
    out = _no_rows[]
    for r in rows
        if len[r.subs] gt 0 and depth lt most
            r.subs = _from_leaves[r.subs, fates, ids, depth + 1, most]
            if len[r.subs] gt 0 or _passes[fates, r.id, ids]
                out = push[out, r]
        else
            if _passes[fates, r.id, ids]
                out = push[out, r]
    out

--- Rows filtered as the table filters: from the root down (a row that
--- fails takes its sub-rows with it, and a row that passes keeps those of
--- them that pass), or from the leaves up (a row stays while it passes or
--- any row under it stays), down to the table's filter depth; below it a
--- row's sub-rows stay whole.
fn _filtered_rows[t, rows, fates, ids]
    kept = _from_root[rows, fates, ids, 0, t.options.filter_depth]
    if t.options.from_leaves
        kept = _from_leaves[rows, fates, ids, 0, t.options.filter_depth]
    kept = _ranked[kept, fates]
    Model[kept, _flattened[kept]]

--- The rows the table's filters keep, as TanStack's filtered row model
--- holds them: each row that a fuzzy filter ranked carries its ranks.
fn filtered_model[t, core]
    if t.options.manual_filtering or len[core.rows] eq 0 or not _filters_on[t.state]
        return core
    fates = fates_of[t, core]
    _filtered_rows[t, core.rows, fates, fates.ids]

-------------------------------------------------------------------- faceting

--- The rows a column's facets count: the data's rows that pass every
--- filter but the column's own (`id` '__global__' for the global filter's
--- facets: every column filter, and not the global one).
fn faceted_rows[t, core, id]
    s = t.state
    if len[core.rows] eq 0 or not _filters_on[s]
        return core
    ids = _no_words[]
    for f in s.filters
        if f.id ne id
            ids = push[ids, f.id]
    if _search_on[s] and id ne '__global__'
        ids = push[ids, '__global__']
    if len[ids] eq 0
        return core
    _filtered_rows[t, core.rows, fates_of[t, core], ids]

fn _facet_columns[t, core, id]
    if id eq '__global__'
        return search_columns[t, core]
    [column_of[t, id]]

fn _no_facets[]
    one = [Facet[missing[], 0]]
    slice[one, 0, 0]

--- The values a column holds over its faceted rows, each once in the order
--- first met and with how many rows hold it (values the same as JavaScript's
--- Map finds them: a list is never the same as another).
fn faceted_values[t, core, id]
    columns = _facet_columns[t, core, id]
    out = _no_facets[]
    for r in faceted_rows[t, core, id].flat
        for c in columns
            v = value_of[r, c]
            k = 0
            found = false
            while k lt len[out] and not found
                if same_value[out[k].value, v]
                    found = true
                else
                    k = k + 1
            if found
                f = out[k]
                f.count = f.count + 1
                out[k] = f
            else
                out = push[out, Facet[v, 1]]
    out

--- The least and greatest numbers a column holds over its faceted rows,
--- each value read as JavaScript's Number() reads it (null as 0, a truth as
--- 1 or 0, a date as its milliseconds; NaN passed over).
fn faceted_range[t, core, id]
    columns = _facet_columns[t, core, id]
    least = _infinity[]
    greatest = 0.0 - _infinity[]
    found = false
    for r in faceted_rows[t, core, id].flat
        for c in columns
            x = js_number[value_of[r, c]]
            if not _is_nan[x]
                found = true
                if x lt least
                    least = x
                if x gt greatest
                    greatest = x
    Range[found, least, greatest]

-------------------------------------------------------------------- grouping and aggregation

fn _range_kind[v]
    if v.is eq 'number'
        return 'number'
    if v.is eq 'date' and not _is_nan[v.num]
        return 'date'
    ''

--- What an aggregation function makes of values (each row's in a column):
--- 'sum' (numbers, anything else as 0), 'min', 'max' and 'extent' (of
--- numbers or of dates, whichever comes first; the extent is a list of
--- two), 'mean' (of what reads as a number, nothing passed over),
--- 'median' (of numbers), 'unique' (each value once, a list),
--- 'uniqueCount', 'count' (of rows), 'first' and 'last'. Missing for a
--- name it does not know, and for a mean or median of nothing.
fn aggregated[name, vs]
    if name eq 'sum'
        total = 0.0
        for v in vs
            if v.is eq 'number'
                total = total + v.num
        return number[total]
    if name eq 'min' or name eq 'max' or name eq 'extent'
        kind_of = ''
        low = missing[]
        high = missing[]
        for v in vs
            k = _range_kind[v]
            if k ne '' and (kind_of eq '' or k eq kind_of)
                if kind_of eq ''
                    kind_of = k
                    low = v
                    high = v
                else
                    if v.num - low.num lt 0.0
                        low = v
                    if v.num - high.num gt 0.0
                        high = v
        if name eq 'min'
            return low
        if name eq 'max'
            return high
        return listed[[low, high]]
    if name eq 'mean'
        count = 0
        total = 0.0
        for v in vs
            if not _blank[v]
                x = js_number[v]
                if not _is_nan[x]
                    count = count + 1
                    total = total + x
        if count eq 0
            return missing[]
        return number[total / count]
    if name eq 'median'
        held = []
        for v in vs
            if v.is eq 'number'
                held = push[held, v.num]
        if len[held] eq 0
            return missing[]
        held = sort[held]
        mid = len[held] / 2
        if len[held] % 2 eq 1
            return number[held[mid]]
        return number[(held[mid - 1] + held[mid]) / 2.0]
    if name eq 'unique' or name eq 'uniqueCount'
        seen = _no_values[]
        for v in vs
            if not _holds[listed[seen], v]
                seen = push[seen, v]
        if name eq 'unique'
            return listed[seen]
        return number[len[seen]]
    if name eq 'count'
        return number[len[vs]]
    if name eq 'first'
        if len[vs] eq 0
            return missing[]
        return vs[0]
    if name eq 'last'
        if len[vs] eq 0
            return missing[]
        return vs[len[vs] - 1]
    missing[]

--- Whether an aggregation function sums up a group from its sub-groups'
--- answers rather than from its rows.
fn _merges[name]
    name in ['sum', 'min', 'max', 'extent', 'count', 'first', 'last']

fn _merged[name, vs]
    if name eq 'sum' or name eq 'count'
        total = 0.0
        for v in vs
            if v.is eq 'number'
                total = total + v.num
        return number[total]
    if name eq 'min' or name eq 'max'
        kind_of = ''
        best = missing[]
        for v in vs
            k = _range_kind[v]
            if k ne ''
                if kind_of eq ''
                    kind_of = k
                if k eq kind_of
                    if best.is eq 'missing'
                        best = v
                    else
                        if name eq 'min' and v.num - best.num lt 0.0
                            best = v
                        if name eq 'max' and v.num - best.num gt 0.0
                            best = v
        return best
    if name eq 'extent'
        kind_of = ''
        low = missing[]
        high = missing[]
        for e in vs
            if e.is eq 'list' and len[e.items] eq 2
                least = e.items[0]
                most = e.items[1]
                k = _range_kind[least]
                if k ne '' and most.is ne 'missing'
                    if kind_of eq ''
                        kind_of = k
                    if k eq kind_of
                        if low.is eq 'missing'
                            low = least
                            high = most
                        else
                            if least.num - low.num lt 0.0
                                low = least
                            if high.is eq 'missing' or most.num - high.num gt 0.0
                                high = most
        return listed[[low, high]]
    if name eq 'first'
        if len[vs] eq 0
            return missing[]
        return vs[0]
    if len[vs] eq 0
        return missing[]
    vs[len[vs] - 1]

--- The aggregation function a column sums a group up with, '' for none:
--- its own, or for 'auto' a sum when the data's first row holds a number
--- there and the extent when it holds a date.
fn aggregate_name[c, core]
    name = c.aggregate_with
    if name eq 'auto'
        if len[core.flat] eq 0
            return ''
        k = _range_kind[value_of[core.flat[0], c]]
        if k eq 'number'
            return 'sum'
        if k eq 'date'
            return 'extent'
        return ''
    if name in ['sum', 'min', 'max', 'extent', 'mean', 'median', 'unique', 'uniqueCount', 'count', 'first', 'last']
        return name
    ''

--- The grouping's ids that name columns.
fn _grouping_of[t]
    out = _no_words[]
    for id in t.state.grouping
        if has_column[t, id]
            out = push[out, id]
    out

fn _deepened[rows, depth]
    out = _no_rows[]
    for r in rows
        r.depth = depth
        if len[r.subs] gt 0
            r.subs = _deepened[r.subs, depth + 1]
        out = push[out, r]
    out

fn _leaf_rows[rows]
    out = _no_rows[]
    for r in rows
        if len[r.subs] gt 0
            out = concat[out, _leaf_rows[r.subs]]
        else
            out = push[out, r]
    out

fn _grouped_up[t, core, rows, depth, parent, grouping, leaves, names]
    if depth gte len[grouping]
        return _deepened[rows, depth]
    by = column_of[t, grouping[depth]]
    keys_seen = _no_words[]
    for r in rows
        key = shown[value_of[r, by]]
        if not (key in keys_seen)
            keys_seen = push[keys_seen, key]
    out = _no_rows[]
    gi = 0
    while gi lt len[keys_seen]
        key = keys_seen[gi]
        members = _no_rows[]
        for r in rows
            if shown[value_of[r, by]] eq key
                members = push[members, r]
        id = '`by.id`:`key`'
        if parent ne ''
            id = '`parent`>`id`'
        subs = _grouped_up[t, core, members, depth + 1, id, grouping, leaves, names]
        held = _no_rows[]
        for s in subs
            s.parent = id
            held = push[held, s]
        subs = held
        deep = false
        for m in members
            if len[m.subs] gt 0
                deep = true
        under = members
        if deep
            under = _leaf_rows[members]
        leaf_ids = _no_words[]
        for u in under
            leaf_ids = push[leaf_ids, u.id]
        merging = len[subs] gt 0
        for s in subs
            if s.column eq ''
                merging = false
        cells = _no_cells[]
        j = 0
        while j lt len[leaves]
            c = leaves[j]
            place = _word_index[grouping, c.id]
            if place gte 0 and place lte depth
                v = value_of[members[0], c]
                if v.is eq 'null'
                    v = missing[]
                cells = set[cells, c.key, v]
            else
                name = names[j]
                if name ne ''
                    if merging and _merges[name] and _not_grouped_by[subs, c.id]
                        answers = _no_values[]
                        for s in subs
                            answers = push[answers, value_of[s, c]]
                        cells = set[cells, c.key, _merged[name, answers]]
                    else
                        vs = _no_values[]
                        for m in members
                            vs = push[vs, value_of[m, c]]
                        cells = set[cells, c.key, aggregated[name, vs]]
            j = j + 1
        out = push[out, Row[id, gi, depth, parent, cells, subs, by.id, key, leaf_ids, _no_ranks[]]]
        gi = gi + 1
    out

fn _not_grouped_by[subs, id]
    for s in subs
        if s.column eq id
            return false
    true

--- The rows the grouping makes, as TanStack's grouped row model holds them:
--- a group row for each value of the first grouping column (in the order
--- first met) holding its rows' groups by the next column, down to the rows
--- themselves; its id is the column and value (`status:single`, under its
--- parent's: `status:single>active:true`), and its cells are its first
--- row's value in the columns it groups by and the columns' aggregates in
--- the rest.
fn grouped_model[t, core, filtered]
    if t.options.manual_grouping or len[filtered.rows] eq 0 or len[t.state.grouping] eq 0
        return filtered
    grouping = _grouping_of[t]
    leaves = _leaves_of[t.columns]
    names = _no_words[]
    for c in leaves
        names = push[names, aggregate_name[c, core]]
    rows = _grouped_up[t, core, filtered.rows, 0, '', grouping, leaves, names]
    Model[rows, _flattened[rows]]

--- What a column's footer sums up: its aggregation over the filtered rows at the top.
fn footer_value[t, m, id]
    c = column_of[t, id]
    name = aggregate_name[c, m.core]
    if name eq ''
        return missing[]
    vs = _no_values[]
    for r in m.filtered.rows
        vs = push[vs, value_of[r, c]]
    aggregated[name, vs]

-------------------------------------------------------------------- sorting rows

--- One column of a sort as the sorted row model resolves it.
struct Sorter [column, desc, absent, invert, by]

fn _no_sorters[]
    one = [Sorter[_nameless[], false, 'greater', false, 'basic']]
    slice[one, 0, 0]

fn _sort_names[]
    ['alphanumeric', 'alphanumericCaseSensitive', 'text', 'textCaseSensitive', 'datetime', 'basic', 'rank']

--- The sort function a column sorts with: its own ('basic' for a name not
--- known), or for 'auto' one chosen by the first ten filtered rows' values:
--- 'datetime' for a date, 'alphanumeric' for text with a digit in it,
--- 'text' for other text, and 'basic' otherwise.
fn sort_name[c, filtered]
    if c.sort_with ne 'auto'
        if c.sort_with in _sort_names[]
            return c.sort_with
        return 'basic'
    worded = false
    i = 0
    while i lt len[filtered.flat] and i lt 10
        v = value_of[filtered.flat[i], c]
        if v.is eq 'date'
            return 'datetime'
        if v.is eq 'text'
            worded = true
            for ch in split[v.words, '']
                if _is_digit[ch]
                    return 'alphanumeric'
        i = i + 1
    if worded
        return 'text'
    'basic'

fn _sorters[t, filtered]
    out = _no_sorters[]
    for s in t.state.sorting
        c = column_of[t, s.id]
        if c.id ne '' and _can_sort[t, c]
            out = push[out, Sorter[c, s.desc, c.absent, c.invert, sort_name[c, filtered]]]
    out

fn _by_rank[a, b, c]
    if c.id in a.ranks
        other = 0.0
        if c.id in b.ranks
            other = b.ranks[c.id]
        n = rank_compare[a.ranks[c.id], other]
        if n ne 0
            return n
    sort_compare['alphanumeric', value_of[a, c], value_of[b, c]]

fn _rows_compare[sorters, a, b]
    i = 0
    while i lt len[sorters]
        s = sorters[i]
        n = 0
        skip = false
        if s.absent ne 'compared'
            gone_a = value_of[a, s.column].is eq 'missing'
            gone_b = value_of[b, s.column].is eq 'missing'
            if gone_a and gone_b
                skip = true
            else if gone_a or gone_b
                if s.absent eq 'first'
                    if gone_a
                        return -1
                    return 1
                if s.absent eq 'last'
                    if gone_a
                        return 1
                    return -1
                n = 1
                if s.absent eq 'smaller'
                    n = -1
                if gone_b
                    n = 0 - n
        if not skip
            if n eq 0
                if s.by eq 'rank'
                    n = _by_rank[a, b, s.column]
                else
                    n = sort_compare[s.by, value_of[a, s.column], value_of[b, s.column]]
            if n ne 0
                if s.desc
                    n = 0 - n
                if s.invert
                    n = 0 - n
                return n
        i = i + 1
    a.index - b.index

fn _merge_sorted[sorters, rows]
    if len[rows] lte 1
        return rows
    half = len[rows] / 2
    left = _merge_sorted[sorters, slice[rows, 0, half]]
    right = _merge_sorted[sorters, slice[rows, half, len[rows]]]
    out = _no_rows[]
    i = 0
    j = 0
    while i lt len[left] and j lt len[right]
        if _rows_compare[sorters, right[j], left[i]] lt 0
            out = push[out, right[j]]
            j = j + 1
        else
            out = push[out, left[i]]
            i = i + 1
    while i lt len[left]
        out = push[out, left[i]]
        i = i + 1
    while j lt len[right]
        out = push[out, right[j]]
        j = j + 1
    out

fn _sorted_rows[sorters, rows]
    out = _no_rows[]
    for r in _merge_sorted[sorters, rows]
        if len[r.subs] gt 0
            r.subs = _sorted_rows[sorters, r.subs]
        out = push[out, r]
    out

--- The rows sorted as the table's sorting says, as TanStack's sorted row
--- model holds them: by each sorting column in turn (rows missing a value
--- placed as the column says), then in their own order; every row's
--- sub-rows sorted the same way.
fn sorted_model[t, filtered, grouped]
    if t.options.manual_sorting or len[grouped.rows] eq 0
        return grouped
    sorters = _sorters[t, filtered]
    if len[sorters] eq 0
        return grouped
    rows = _sorted_rows[sorters, grouped.rows]
    Model[rows, _flattened[rows]]

-------------------------------------------------------------------- expanding and pages

fn _is_open[t, id]
    t.state.expanded_all or (id in t.state.expanded and t.state.expanded[id])

fn _opened[t, rows]
    out = _no_rows[]
    for r in rows
        out = push[out, r]
        if len[r.subs] gt 0 and _is_open[t, r.id]
            out = concat[out, _opened[t, r.subs]]
    out

fn _any_expanded[s]
    s.expanded_all or len[keys[s.expanded]] gt 0

--- The rows with the expanded ones' sub-rows listed after them, as
--- TanStack's expanded row model holds them (only when expanded rows are
--- paged with the rest, or the pages are the program's own).
fn expanded_model[t, sorted]
    if t.options.manual_expanding or len[sorted.rows] eq 0 or not _any_expanded[t.state]
        return sorted
    if not t.options.page_expanded and not t.options.manual_pages
        return sorted
    Model[_opened[t, sorted.rows], sorted.flat]

fn _page_flat[rows]
    out = _no_rows[]
    seen = _no_flags[]
    for r in _flattened[rows]
        if not (r.id in seen)
            seen = set[seen, r.id, true]
            out = push[out, r]
    out

--- The page's rows, as TanStack's paginated row model holds them: the
--- page's share of the rows (with the expanded rows' sub-rows opened inside
--- the page when expanded rows are not paged with the rest), and every row
--- on the page and under it once.
fn page_model[t, pre]
    if t.options.manual_pages or len[pre.rows] eq 0
        return pre
    size = t.state.page_size
    head = max[0, min[size * t.state.page_index, len[pre.rows]]]
    foot = max[head, min[head + size, len[pre.rows]]]
    rows = slice[pre.rows, head, foot]
    if not t.options.page_expanded
        rows = _opened[t, rows]
    Model[rows, _page_flat[rows]]

--- Every row model the table's state makes, each from the one before.
fn models[t]
    core = core_model[t]
    filtered = filtered_model[t, core]
    grouped = grouped_model[t, core, filtered]
    sorted = sorted_model[t, filtered, grouped]
    opened = expanded_model[t, sorted]
    page = page_model[t, opened]
    Models[core, filtered, grouped, sorted, opened, page, _positions[grouped.flat], _positions[core.flat]]

--- The row of an id as the table finds one for a change: the grouped rows'
--- (a group's too) or else the data's; a row with no id when there is none.
fn row_of[m, id]
    if id in m.held
        return m.grouped.flat[m.held[id]]
    if id in m.found
        return m.core.flat[m.found[id]]
    _unfound[]

--- The data's row of an id, its sub-rows whole; a row with no id when there is none.
fn data_row_of[m, id]
    if id in m.found
        return m.core.flat[m.found[id]]
    _unfound[]

-------------------------------------------------------------------- the table

fn _no_sorts[]
    one = [Sort['', false]]
    slice[one, 0, 0]

fn _no_filters[]
    one = [Filter['', missing[]]]
    slice[one, 0, 0]

--- A table's options, TanStack's under plain names, each with its default:
--- `sortable` (enableSorting), `multi` (enableMultiSort: 'auto' lets every
--- column that reads a cell join a sort of several, 'yes', 'no'),
--- `removable` (enableSortingRemoval), `multi_removable`
--- (enableMultiRemove), `most_sorted` (maxMultiSortColCount, -1 for no
--- limit), `first` (sortDescFirst: 'auto', 'asc' or 'desc'),
--- `reset_sorting` (autoResetSorting: whether new data takes the sorting
--- back to the state the table began with), `filtering` (enableFilters),
--- `column_filtering` (enableColumnFilters), `searching`
--- (enableGlobalFilter), `search_filter` (globalFilterFn), `from_leaves`
--- (filterFromLeafRows), `filter_depth` (maxLeafRowFilterDepth),
--- `groupable` (enableGrouping), `grouped_columns` (groupedColumnMode:
--- 'reorder', 'remove' or 'keep'), `expandable` (enableExpanding),
--- `page_expanded` (paginateExpandedRows), `reset_expanded`
--- (autoResetExpanded: 'auto', 'yes', 'no'), `known_pages` (pageCount: -2
--- counts the rows, -1 is unknown), `known_rows` (rowCount, -1 counts the
--- rows), `reset_pages` (autoResetPageIndex: 'auto', 'yes', 'no'),
--- `selectable` (enableRowSelection), `multi_select`
--- (enableMultiRowSelection), `select_subs` (enableSubRowSelection),
--- `row_pinnable` (enableRowPinning), `keep_pinned` (keepPinnedRows),
--- `hideable` (enableHiding), `column_pinnable` (enableColumnPinning),
--- `resizable` (enableColumnResizing), `resize_mode` (columnResizeMode:
--- 'end' or 'change') and `resize_direction` ('ltr' or 'rtl'); and the
--- manual flags, by which the program does a step itself (`manual_sorting`,
--- `manual_filtering`, `manual_grouping`, `manual_expanding`,
--- `manual_pages`). Three lists name rows by id where TanStack takes a
--- function of the row: `unselectable` (rows that cannot be selected),
--- `closed` (rows whose sub-rows are not selected with them) and
--- `unpinnable` (rows that cannot be pinned); set them on the record.
fn options[sortable = true, multi = 'auto', removable = true, multi_removable = true, most_sorted = -1, first = 'auto', manual_sorting = false, reset_sorting = false, filtering = true, column_filtering = true, searching = true, search_filter = 'auto', from_leaves = false, filter_depth = 100, manual_filtering = false, groupable = true, grouped_columns = 'reorder', manual_grouping = false, expandable = true, page_expanded = true, manual_expanding = false, reset_expanded = 'auto', manual_pages = false, known_pages = -2, known_rows = -1, reset_pages = 'auto', selectable = true, multi_select = true, select_subs = true, row_pinnable = true, keep_pinned = true, hideable = true, column_pinnable = true, resizable = true, resize_mode = 'end', resize_direction = 'ltr']
    Options[sortable, multi, removable, multi_removable, most_sorted, first, manual_sorting, reset_sorting, filtering, column_filtering, searching, search_filter, from_leaves, filter_depth, manual_filtering, groupable, grouped_columns, manual_grouping, expandable, page_expanded, manual_expanding, reset_expanded, manual_pages, known_pages, known_rows, reset_pages, selectable, _no_words[], multi_select, select_subs, _no_words[], row_pinnable, _no_words[], keep_pinned, hideable, column_pinnable, resizable, resize_mode, resize_direction]

--- An empty state: nothing sorted, filtered, grouped, expanded, selected,
--- hidden, ordered, pinned or sized, on the first page of ten rows.
fn empty_state[]
    State[_no_sorts[], _no_filters[], missing[], _no_words[], false, _no_flags[], 0, 10, _no_flags[], _no_flags[], _no_words[], _no_words[], _no_words[], _no_words[], _no_words[], _no_sizes[], _still[]]

--- A table of these columns and rows, with the default options and an empty state.
fn new[columns, rows]
    Table[columns, rows, options[], empty_state[], empty_state[]]

--- A table with these options and this state to begin with (the state an
--- automatic reset of the expanded rows goes back to).
fn made[columns, rows, o, s]
    Table[columns, rows, o, s, s]

--- The table with new rows of data: the expanded rows go back to the state
--- the table began with, the page to the first, and the sorting too when
--- the table resets it.
fn rows_set[t, rows]
    t.rows = rows
    t = _expanded_reset[t]
    t = _page_reset[t]
    if t.options.reset_sorting
        t.state.sorting = t.initial.sorting
    t

--- The table with new column definitions.
fn columns_set[t, columns]
    t.columns = columns
    t

fn _expanded_reset[t]
    r = t.options.reset_expanded
    if r eq 'yes' or (r eq 'auto' and not t.options.manual_expanding)
        t.state.expanded_all = t.initial.expanded_all
        t.state.expanded = t.initial.expanded
    t

fn _page_reset[t]
    r = t.options.reset_pages
    if (r eq 'yes' or (r eq 'auto' and not t.options.manual_pages)) and t.state.page_index ne 0
        return page_set[t, 0]
    t

-------------------------------------------------------------------- sorting

fn _can_sort[t, c]
    c.sortable and t.options.sortable and c.key ne ''

--- Whether a column may sort: it says so, the table does, and it reads a cell.
fn can_sort[t, id]
    _can_sort[t, column_of[t, id]]

fn _can_multi_sort[t, c]
    if c.multi ne 'auto'
        return c.multi eq 'yes'
    if t.options.multi ne 'auto'
        return t.options.multi eq 'yes'
    c.key ne ''

--- Whether a column may join a sort of several columns.
fn can_multi_sort[t, id]
    _can_multi_sort[t, column_of[t, id]]

--- How a column is sorted: 'asc', 'desc' or '' (not at all).
fn sort_of[t, id]
    for s in t.state.sorting
        if s.id eq id
            if s.desc
                return 'desc'
            return 'asc'
    ''

--- Where a column stands in the sorting, -1 when it does not.
fn sort_index[t, id]
    i = 0
    while i lt len[t.state.sorting]
        if t.state.sorting[i].id eq id
            return i
        i = i + 1
    -1

fn _auto_sort[c, filtered]
    i = 0
    while i lt len[filtered.flat] and i lt 10
        v = value_of[filtered.flat[i], c]
        if not _blank[v]
            if v.is eq 'text'
                return 'asc'
            return 'desc'
        i = i + 1
    'desc'

--- The direction a column's values ask for: ascending for text, descending
--- for anything else, as the first of the first ten filtered rows that holds
--- a value (not missing or null) says; descending when none does.
fn auto_sort[t, m, id]
    _auto_sort[column_of[t, id], m.filtered]

fn _first_sort[t, c, filtered]
    if c.first ne 'auto'
        return c.first
    if t.options.first ne 'auto'
        return t.options.first
    _auto_sort[c, filtered]

--- The direction a column sorts in first: its own, the table's, or the one its values ask for.
fn first_sort[t, m, id]
    _first_sort[t, column_of[t, id], m.filtered]

fn _next_sort[t, c, filtered, multi]
    first = _first_sort[t, c, filtered]
    current = sort_of[t, c.id]
    if current eq ''
        return first
    if current ne first and t.options.removable and (not multi or t.options.multi_removable)
        return ''
    if current eq 'desc'
        return 'asc'
    'desc'

--- The direction a press would sort a column in next ('' to stop sorting
--- by it): unsorted it takes its first direction; in its first direction it
--- turns round; in the other it stops, unless the table keeps sorting (or,
--- in a sort of several, multi_removable says no), when it turns round.
fn next_sort[t, m, id, multi]
    _next_sort[t, column_of[t, id], m.filtered, multi]

--- The sort function a column sorts with, as sort_name chooses it.
fn sort_function[t, m, id]
    sort_name[column_of[t, id], m.filtered]

--- The table sorted by these columns in turn, its sorting replaced; the
--- page goes back to the first.
fn sorted_by[t, sorting]
    t.state.sorting = sorting
    if t.options.manual_sorting
        return t
    _page_reset[t]

--- The table after a press on a column's sort, as TanStack's sorting
--- toggle answers it (`multi` when the press asks for a sort of several, a
--- shift-press): a column that cannot sort is left as it is; alone, the
--- column replaces the sorting or turns round; in a sort of several it is
--- added at the end (the oldest dropped past most_sorted) or turns round;
--- and a column whose next direction is none leaves the sorting.
fn sort_toggled[t, id, multi]
    c = column_of[t, id]
    if not _can_sort[t, c]
        return t
    many = multi and _can_multi_sort[t, c]
    filtered = filtered_model[t, core_model[t]]
    next = _next_sort[t, c, filtered, many]
    old = t.state.sorting
    at = sort_index[t, id]
    several = len[old] gt 0 and many
    desc = next eq 'desc'
    out = _no_sorts[]
    if at lt 0
        if several
            out = push[old, Sort[id, desc]]
            most = t.options.most_sorted
            if most gte 0 and len[out] gt most
                out = slice[out, len[out] - most, len[out]]
        else
            out = push[out, Sort[id, desc]]
    else if next eq ''
        if several
            for s in old
                if s.id ne id
                    out = push[out, s]
    else if several
        for s in old
            if s.id eq id
                out = push[out, Sort[id, desc]]
            else
                out = push[out, s]
    else
        out = push[out, Sort[id, desc]]
    sorted_by[t, out]

-------------------------------------------------------------------- filtering

--- Whether a column may be filtered: it says so, the table lets columns
--- filter, and it reads a cell. (A filter set on one that may not still
--- filters.)
fn can_filter[t, id]
    c = column_of[t, id]
    c.filterable and t.options.column_filtering and t.options.filtering and c.key ne ''

--- Whether the global filter reads a column.
fn can_search[t, m, id]
    _searchable[t, column_of[t, id], m.core]

--- The filter function a column filters with ('' for none).
fn filter_function[t, m, id]
    _filter_name[column_of[t, id], m.core]

--- The value a column is filtered by, missing when it is not.
fn filter_value[t, id]
    for f in t.state.filters
        if f.id eq id
            return f.value
    missing[]

--- Whether a column is filtered.
fn is_filtered[t, id]
    for f in t.state.filters
        if f.id eq id
            return true
    false

fn _filters_changed[t, filters, search]
    before = _filters_on[t.state]
    t.state.filters = filters
    t.state.search = search
    if t.options.manual_filtering
        return t
    if len[t.rows] gt 0 and (before or _filters_on[t.state]) and not t.options.manual_grouping
        t = _expanded_reset[t]
    _page_reset[t]

--- The table filtered by these column filters, its filters replaced: a
--- filter on a leaf column whose value takes it away (filter_removes says)
--- is dropped. The page goes back to the first, and the expanded rows to
--- the state the table began with.
fn filters_set[t, filters]
    core = core_model[t]
    leaves = leaf_columns[t]
    kept = _no_filters[]
    for f in filters
        k = _column_index[leaves, f.id]
        gone = false
        if k gte 0
            gone = filter_removes[_filter_name[leaves[k], core], f.value]
        if not gone
            kept = push[kept, f]
    _filters_changed[t, kept, t.state.search]

--- The table with one column's filter set to a value, or taken away when
--- the value takes it away; the other filters keep theirs.
fn filter_set[t, id, value]
    gone = filter_removes[_filter_name[column_of[t, id], core_model[t]], value]
    out = _no_filters[]
    found = false
    for f in t.state.filters
        if f.id eq id
            found = true
            if not gone
                out = push[out, Filter[id, value]]
        else
            out = push[out, f]
    if not found and not gone
        out = push[out, Filter[id, value]]
    filters_set[t, out]

--- The table searched by a global filter value (missing, null or '' for
--- none); as a filter's change, the page goes back to the first and the
--- expanded rows to the state the table began with.
fn search_set[t, value]
    old = t.state.search
    if old.is ne 'list' and strictly_same[old, value]
        return t
    _filters_changed[t, t.state.filters, value]

-------------------------------------------------------------------- grouping

--- Whether a column may group: it says so, the table does, and it reads a cell.
fn can_group[t, id]
    c = column_of[t, id]
    c.groupable and t.options.groupable and c.key ne ''

--- Whether the table is grouped by a column.
fn is_grouped[t, id]
    id in t.state.grouping

--- Where a column stands in the grouping, -1 when it does not.
fn grouped_index[t, id]
    _word_index[t.state.grouping, id]

--- The table grouped by these columns in turn, its grouping replaced: the
--- page goes back to the first, and the expanded rows to the state the
--- table began with.
fn grouping_set[t, ids]
    t.state.grouping = ids
    if t.options.manual_grouping
        return t
    t = _expanded_reset[t]
    _page_reset[t]

--- The table with a column added to the end of the grouping, or taken out of it.
fn grouping_toggled[t, id]
    if id in t.state.grouping
        return grouping_set[t, _without[t.state.grouping, [id]]]
    grouping_set[t, push[t.state.grouping, id]]

--- Whether a row's cell in a column holds its group's value: the row groups by that column.
fn cell_grouped[t, r, id]
    is_grouped[t, id] and r.column eq id

--- Whether a row's cell in a grouped column holds no value of its own (the
--- group above it shows it).
fn cell_placeholder[t, r, id]
    is_grouped[t, id] and r.column ne id

--- Whether a row's cell holds an aggregate: the row is a group's, by another
--- column, and this column is not grouped and has an aggregation function.
fn cell_aggregated[t, m, r, id]
    r.column ne '' and r.column ne id and not is_grouped[t, id] and aggregate_name[column_of[t, id], m.core] ne ''

-------------------------------------------------------------------- expanding

--- Whether a row may expand: the table lets rows expand and it has rows under it.
fn can_expand[t, r]
    t.options.expandable and len[r.subs] gt 0

--- Whether a row is expanded.
fn is_expanded[t, id]
    _is_open[t, id]

--- Whether every row above a row is expanded (a row at the top: yes).
fn parents_expanded[t, m, id]
    parent = row_of[m, id].parent
    while parent ne ''
        if not _is_open[t, parent]
            return false
        parent = row_of[m, parent].parent
    true

--- Whether the state expands every row, or every row that can expand on the page is expanded.
fn all_expanded[t, m]
    if t.state.expanded_all
        return true
    if len[keys[t.state.expanded]] eq 0
        return false
    openable = false
    for r in m.page.flat
        if can_expand[t, r]
            openable = true
            if not _is_open[t, r.id]
                return false
    openable

--- Whether any row is expanded.
fn some_expanded[t]
    if t.state.expanded_all
        return true
    for id in keys[t.state.expanded]
        if t.state.expanded[id]
            return true
    false

--- Whether any row the pages hold can expand.
fn can_some_expand[t, m]
    for r in m.expanded.flat
        if can_expand[t, r]
            return true
    false

--- How deep the expanded rows reach, counted in the parts of their ids
--- (TanStack's rule: `t3.0` is 2): the expanded rows', or when every row is
--- expanded, every row's that can expand.
fn expanded_depth[t, m]
    most = 0
    if t.state.expanded_all
        for r in m.grouped.flat
            if can_expand[t, r]
                most = max[most, len[split[r.id, '.']]]
        return most
    for id in keys[t.state.expanded]
        most = max[most, len[split[id, '.']]]
    most

--- The table with its expanded state set: every row (`all`), or the rows
--- whose ids `opened` holds true.
fn expansion_set[t, all_open, opened]
    t.state.expanded_all = all_open
    t.state.expanded = opened
    t

--- The table with a row expanded or collapsed (`open`): a row expands only
--- when it can; when every row was expanded, each that can stays expanded
--- but this one.
fn row_expansion_set[t, id, open]
    was_open = _is_open[t, id]
    if open eq was_open
        return t
    m = models[t]
    if open and not can_expand[t, row_of[m, id]]
        return t
    opened = _no_flags[]
    if t.state.expanded_all
        for r in m.grouped.flat
            if can_expand[t, r]
                opened = set[opened, r.id, true]
    else
        for k in keys[t.state.expanded]
            if t.state.expanded[k]
                opened = set[opened, k, true]
    if open
        opened = set[opened, id, true]
    else if id in opened
        opened = del[opened, id]
    expansion_set[t, false, opened]

--- The table with a row expanded if it was collapsed and collapsed if it was expanded.
fn row_expansion_toggled[t, id]
    row_expansion_set[t, id, not _is_open[t, id]]

--- The table with every row expanded (when any can) or none.
fn all_expansion_set[t, open]
    if open
        if t.state.expanded_all
            return t
        if not can_some_expand[t, models[t]]
            return t
        return expansion_set[t, true, _no_flags[]]
    if not t.state.expanded_all and len[keys[t.state.expanded]] eq 0
        return t
    expansion_set[t, false, _no_flags[]]

--- The table with every row expanded unless all were, then none.
fn all_expansion_toggled[t]
    all_expansion_set[t, not all_expanded[t, models[t]]]

-------------------------------------------------------------------- pages

--- How many rows the pages share: the table's row count, or the rows to page.
fn row_count[t, m]
    if t.options.known_rows gte 0
        return t.options.known_rows
    len[m.expanded.rows]

--- How many pages there are: the table's page count (-1 when it is not
--- known), or the rows to page over the page size, rounded up.
fn page_count[t, m]
    if t.options.known_pages ne -2
        return t.options.known_pages
    rows = row_count[t, m]
    size = t.state.page_size
    (rows + size - 1) / size

--- Whether there is a page before this one.
fn can_previous[t]
    t.state.page_index gt 0

--- Whether there is a page after this one (always, when the count is not known).
fn can_next[t, m]
    count = page_count[t, m]
    if count eq -1
        return true
    if count eq 0
        return false
    t.state.page_index lt count - 1

--- Whether there is a last page to go to after this one.
fn can_last[t, m]
    count = page_count[t, m]
    count gt 0 and t.state.page_index lt count - 1

--- The pages' indexes, 0 up to the page count (none when it is not known).
fn page_numbers[t, m]
    out = []
    i = 0
    count = page_count[t, m]
    while i lt count
        out = push[out, i]
        i = i + 1
    out

--- The table on a page, kept from 0 up to the last page the table's page
--- count allows (any, when it counts its rows or the count is not known).
fn page_set[t, index]
    most = 9007199254740991
    if t.options.known_pages gte 0
        most = t.options.known_pages - 1
    t.state.page_index = max[0, min[index, most]]
    t

--- The table with pages of a new size (1 at least): the page is the one
--- that holds the row the old page began with.
fn page_size_set[t, size]
    fresh = max[1, size]
    t.state.page_index = (t.state.page_size * t.state.page_index) / fresh
    t.state.page_size = fresh
    t

--- The table on the next page.
fn next_page[t]
    page_set[t, t.state.page_index + 1]

--- The table on the page before.
fn previous_page[t]
    page_set[t, t.state.page_index - 1]

--- The table on the first page.
fn first_page[t]
    page_set[t, 0]

--- The table on the last page, when the page count is known and not 0.
fn last_page[t]
    count = page_count[t, models[t]]
    if count lte 0
        return t
    page_set[t, count - 1]

-------------------------------------------------------------------- selection

--- Whether a row may be selected: the table lets rows select and does not name it unselectable.
fn can_select[t, id]
    t.options.selectable and not (id in t.options.unselectable)

--- Whether selecting a row leaves the others selected.
fn can_multi_select[t]
    t.options.multi_select

--- Whether selecting a row selects the rows under it.
fn can_select_subs[t, id]
    t.options.select_subs and not (id in t.options.closed)

--- Whether a row is selected.
fn is_selected[t, id]
    id in t.state.selected and t.state.selected[id]

fn _subs_selected[t, r]
    if len[r.subs] eq 0
        return ''
    some = false
    every = true
    selectable = false
    i = 0
    while i lt len[r.subs] and not (some and not every)
        s = r.subs[i]
        if can_select[t, s.id]
            selectable = true
            if is_selected[t, s.id]
                some = true
            else
                every = false
        if len[s.subs] gt 0
            under = _subs_selected[t, s]
            if under eq 'all'
                some = true
                selectable = true
            else if under eq 'some'
                some = true
                every = false
                selectable = true
            else
                every = false
        i = i + 1
    if not selectable
        return ''
    if every
        return 'all'
    if some
        return 'some'
    ''

--- How a row's sub-rows are selected: 'all', 'some' or '' (none, or none can be).
fn subs_selected[t, r]
    _subs_selected[t, r]

fn _selected_into[t, m, chosen, id, value, children, respect]
    out = chosen
    if value
        if not can_multi_select[t]
            out = _no_flags[]
        if can_select[t, id]
            out = set[out, id, true]
    else if not respect or can_select[t, id]
        if id in out
            out = del[out, id]
    r = row_of[m, id]
    if children and len[r.subs] gt 0 and can_select_subs[t, id]
        for s in r.subs
            out = _selected_into[t, m, out, s.id, value, children, respect]
    out

fn _selectable_in_all[t, m, id]
    if not can_select[t, id]
        return false
    if t.options.select_subs and len[t.options.closed] eq 0
        return true
    parent = row_of[m, id].parent
    while parent ne ''
        if not can_select_subs[t, parent]
            return false
        parent = row_of[m, parent].parent
    true

--- The table with its selection set to these rows.
fn selection_set[t, ids]
    chosen = _no_flags[]
    for id in ids
        chosen = set[chosen, id, true]
    t.state.selected = chosen
    t

--- The table with a row selected or not (`value`), and the rows under it
--- with it when it selects several; selecting one where only one may be
--- selected lets the others go.
fn row_selection_set[t, id, value]
    m = models[t]
    t.state.selected = _selected_into[t, m, t.state.selected, id, value, can_multi_select[t], false]
    t

--- The table with a row selected if it was not, and let go if it was.
fn row_selection_toggled[t, id]
    row_selection_set[t, id, not is_selected[t, id]]

--- The table with every filtered row selected that a select-all reaches
--- (it may be selected, and no row above it keeps its sub-rows out), or
--- every one let go that may be.
fn all_selection_set[t, value]
    m = models[t]
    chosen = t.state.selected
    for r in m.filtered.flat
        if value
            if _selectable_in_all[t, m, r.id]
                chosen = set[chosen, r.id, true]
        else if can_select[t, r.id] and r.id in chosen
            chosen = del[chosen, r.id]
    t.state.selected = chosen
    t

--- The table with every row selected unless all were, then none.
fn all_selection_toggled[t]
    all_selection_set[t, not all_selected[t, models[t]]]

--- The table with the page's rows selected or not, with the rows under them.
fn page_selection_set[t, value]
    m = models[t]
    chosen = t.state.selected
    for r in m.page.rows
        chosen = _selected_into[t, m, chosen, r.id, value, true, true]
    t.state.selected = chosen
    t

--- The table with the page's rows selected unless all were, then let go.
fn page_selection_toggled[t]
    page_selection_set[t, not all_page_selected[t, models[t]]]

--- Whether every filtered row that a select-all reaches is selected (and some row is).
fn all_selected[t, m]
    if len[m.filtered.flat] eq 0 or len[keys[t.state.selected]] eq 0
        return false
    for r in m.filtered.flat
        if not is_selected[t, r.id] and _selectable_in_all[t, m, r.id]
            return false
    true

--- Whether any row is selected.
fn some_selected[t]
    len[keys[t.state.selected]] gt 0

--- Whether every row of the page (and under it) that a select-all reaches
--- is selected, and one such is.
fn all_page_selected[t, m]
    seen = false
    for r in m.page.flat
        if not is_selected[t, r.id]
            if _selectable_in_all[t, m, r.id]
                return false
        else if _selectable_in_all[t, m, r.id]
            seen = true
    seen

--- Whether any row of the page that may be selected is, or has rows under it selected.
fn some_page_selected[t, m]
    for r in m.page.flat
        if can_select[t, r.id]
            if is_selected[t, r.id] or _subs_selected[t, r] eq 'some'
                return true
    false

fn _chosen_tree[t, rows]
    out = _no_rows[]
    for r in rows
        if is_selected[t, r.id]
            if len[r.subs] gt 0
                r.subs = _chosen_tree[t, r.subs]
            out = push[out, r]
    out

fn _chosen_flat[t, rows]
    out = _no_rows[]
    for r in rows
        if is_selected[t, r.id]
            out = push[out, r]
        if len[r.subs] gt 0
            out = concat[out, _chosen_flat[t, r.subs]]
    out

--- The selected rows of a row model: `rows` the selected rows at the top
--- with the selected rows under them, `flat` every selected row in order.
fn selected_of[t, model]
    if len[keys[t.state.selected]] eq 0
        return Model[_no_rows[], _no_rows[]]
    Model[_chosen_tree[t, model.rows], _chosen_flat[t, model.rows]]

--- The selected rows of the data.
fn selected_rows[t, m]
    selected_of[t, m.core]

--- The selected rows the filters keep.
fn filtered_selected_rows[t, m]
    selected_of[t, m.filtered]

--- The selected rows as grouped and sorted.
fn grouped_selected_rows[t, m]
    selected_of[t, m.sorted]

-------------------------------------------------------------------- row pinning

--- Whether a row may be pinned: the table lets rows pin and does not name it unpinnable.
fn can_pin_row[t, id]
    t.options.row_pinnable and not (id in t.options.unpinnable)

--- The side a row is pinned to: 'top', 'bottom' or ''.
fn row_pin_of[t, id]
    if id in t.state.top
        return 'top'
    if id in t.state.bottom
        return 'bottom'
    ''

fn _leaves_under[r]
    out = _no_words[]
    for s in r.subs
        out = push[out, s.id]
        if len[s.subs] gt 0
            out = concat[out, _leaves_under[s]]
    out

fn _parents_above[m, id]
    out = _no_words[]
    parent = row_of[m, id].parent
    while parent ne ''
        out = concat[[parent], out]
        parent = row_of[m, parent].parent
    out

--- The table with a row pinned to the top or the bottom ('top', 'bottom'),
--- or freed (''): with every row under it when `leaves`, and with the rows
--- above it when `parents`, after what that side held.
fn row_pinned[t, id, side, leaves, parents]
    m = models[t]
    ids = _no_words[]
    if parents
        ids = _parents_above[m, id]
    ids = push[ids, id]
    if leaves
        for under in _leaves_under[row_of[m, id]]
            if not (under in ids)
                ids = push[ids, under]
    up = _without[t.state.top, ids]
    down = _without[t.state.bottom, ids]
    if side eq 'top'
        up = concat[up, ids]
    if side eq 'bottom'
        down = concat[down, ids]
    t.state.top = up
    t.state.bottom = down
    t

fn _pinned_rows[t, m, side]
    ids = t.state.top
    if side eq 'bottom'
        ids = t.state.bottom
    out = _no_rows[]
    for id in ids
        if t.options.keep_pinned
            r = row_of[m, id]
            if r.id ne '' and parents_expanded[t, m, id]
                out = push[out, r]
        else
            for r in m.page.rows
                if r.id eq id
                    out = push[out, r]
    out

--- The rows pinned to the top, in the order they were pinned: each wherever
--- it stands when the table keeps pinned rows (and its parents are
--- expanded), else only those on the page.
fn top_rows[t, m]
    _pinned_rows[t, m, 'top']

--- The rows pinned to the bottom, as top_rows has them.
fn bottom_rows[t, m]
    _pinned_rows[t, m, 'bottom']

--- The page's rows that are not pinned.
fn center_rows[t, m]
    out = _no_rows[]
    for r in m.page.rows
        if not (r.id in t.state.top) and not (r.id in t.state.bottom)
            out = push[out, r]
    out

--- Where a pinned row stands among the rows shown on its side, -1 when it is
--- not pinned or not shown.
fn pinned_row_index[t, m, id]
    side = row_pin_of[t, id]
    if side eq ''
        return -1
    i = 0
    for r in _pinned_rows[t, m, side]
        if r.id eq id
            return i
        i = i + 1
    -1

--- Whether any row is pinned: to a side ('top' or 'bottom'), or to either ('').
fn some_rows_pinned[t, side = '']
    if side eq 'top'
        return len[t.state.top] gt 0
    if side eq 'bottom'
        return len[t.state.bottom] gt 0
    len[t.state.top] gt 0 or len[t.state.bottom] gt 0

-------------------------------------------------------------------- Grid.js

--- One column of a Grid.js sort: the column's index and its direction (1
--- ascending, -1 descending).
struct GridSort [index, direction]

--- A grid as Grid.js keeps one: its rows (each a list of cells), the
--- search keyword, the sort (the columns in the order pressed), the page
--- shown (from 0), the rows to a page and how many page buttons it shows
--- between the gaps.
struct Grid [rows, keyword, sorts, page, limit, count]

--- A page button of a grid: its words, whether it is turned off, whether it
--- is the page shown, whether it only marks a gap ('...'), its title, and
--- the page it goes to (-1 for none).
struct GridButton [label, disabled, current, spread, title, goes]

--- A grid's words, Grid.js's language: the search field's placeholder, the
--- sort buttons' titles, the page buttons' words and titles (`{page}` and
--- `{pages}` stand for numbers), the summary's words, and what an empty or
--- failed grid says.
struct Language [placeholder, sort_ascending, sort_descending, previous, following, navigate, page_title, first_page, showing, of_words, to_words, results, loading, none_found, failed]

--- Grid.js's own words (its en_US language). Its first page button's
--- title is the key it looks up, which en_US does not hold.
fn grid_language[]
    Language['Type a keyword...', 'Sort column ascending', 'Sort column descending', 'Previous', 'Next', 'Page {page} of {pages}', 'Page {page}', 'pagination.firstPage', 'Showing', 'of', 'to', 'results', 'Loading...', 'No matching records found', 'An error happened while fetching the data']

fn _no_grid_sorts[]
    one = [GridSort[0, 1]]
    slice[one, 0, 0]

--- A grid of these rows on its first page: `limit` rows to a page and
--- `count` page buttons, as Grid.js's pagination has them unless told.
fn grid[rows, limit = 10, count = 3]
    Grid[rows, '', _no_grid_sorts[], 0, limit, count]

fn _canonical_letter[c]
    big = upper[c]
    if len[big] ne 1
        return c
    if code[c] gte 128 and code[big] lt 128
        return c
    big

--- Text as a JavaScript regular expression that ignores case compares it:
--- each letter in upper case, unless that takes more letters than one or
--- turns a letter beyond ASCII into one within it.
fn _canonical[t]
    parts = for c in split[t, '']
        _canonical_letter[c]
    join[parts, '']

fn _grid_text[v]
    if _blank[v]
        if v.is eq 'missing'
            return 'undefined'
        return ''
    shown[v]

--- Whether a row of cells holds a search keyword, as Grid.js's search finds
--- it: in any cell, the keyword's text (trimmed) anywhere in the cell's,
--- whatever the case; an empty keyword holds every row.
fn grid_matches[cells, keyword]
    if keyword eq ''
        return true
    sought = _canonical[trim[keyword]]
    for v in cells
        if sought in _canonical[_grid_text[v]]
            return true
    false

fn _grid_compare[a, b]
    if js_less[b, a]
        return 1
    if js_less[a, b]
        return -1
    0

fn _grid_rows_compare[g, a, b]
    for s in g.sorts
        n = _grid_compare[g.rows[a][s.index], g.rows[b][s.index]] * s.direction
        if n ne 0
            return n
    0

fn _grid_merge[g, order]
    if len[order] lte 1
        return order
    half = len[order] / 2
    left = _grid_merge[g, slice[order, 0, half]]
    right = _grid_merge[g, slice[order, half, len[order]]]
    out = []
    i = 0
    j = 0
    while i lt len[left] and j lt len[right]
        if _grid_rows_compare[g, right[j], left[i]] lt 0
            out = push[out, right[j]]
            j = j + 1
        else
            out = push[out, left[i]]
            i = i + 1
    while i lt len[left]
        out = push[out, left[i]]
        i = i + 1
    while j lt len[right]
        out = push[out, right[j]]
        j = j + 1
    out

--- The rows a grid's search keeps, by their places in its rows, sorted as
--- its sort says (by each sorting column in turn, as JavaScript's < and >
--- compare the cells; ties keep their order).
fn grid_order[g]
    kept = []
    i = 0
    while i lt len[g.rows]
        if grid_matches[g.rows[i], g.keyword]
            kept = push[kept, i]
        i = i + 1
    if len[g.sorts] eq 0
        return kept
    _grid_merge[g, kept]

--- How many rows a grid's search keeps.
fn grid_total[g]
    len[grid_order[g]]

--- How many pages a grid's kept rows fill.
fn grid_pages[g]
    (grid_total[g] + g.limit - 1) / g.limit

--- The rows on a grid's page, by their places in its rows.
fn grid_shown[g]
    order = grid_order[g]
    head = min[g.page * g.limit, len[order]]
    slice[order, head, min[head + g.limit, len[order]]]

--- The grid searched for a keyword; a search goes back to the first page.
fn grid_searched[g, keyword]
    g.keyword = keyword
    g.page = 0
    g

--- The grid after a press on a column's sort button, as Grid.js answers it
--- (`multi` for a shift-press): an unsorted column sorts ascending, a sorted
--- one turns round; alone, the column replaces the sort (or turns round in
--- it when it is the only one); in a sort of several it is added at the
--- end, turns round when ascending, and leaves when descending. A sort goes
--- back to the first page.
fn grid_sort_pressed[g, index, multi]
    at = -1
    i = 0
    while i lt len[g.sorts]
        if g.sorts[i].index eq index
            at = i
        i = i + 1
    direction = 1
    if at gte 0 and g.sorts[at].direction eq 1
        direction = -1
    out = _no_grid_sorts[]
    if at lt 0
        if multi
            out = g.sorts
        out = push[out, GridSort[index, direction]]
    else if not multi
        out = push[out, GridSort[index, direction]]
    else if g.sorts[at].direction eq -1
        j = 0
        while j lt len[g.sorts]
            if j ne at
                out = push[out, g.sorts[j]]
            j = j + 1
    else
        out = g.sorts
        out[at] = GridSort[index, direction]
    g.sorts = out
    g.page = 0
    g

--- The grid on a page, when the page is one of its pages and not the one shown.
fn grid_page_pressed[g, page]
    if page lt 0 or page gte grid_pages[g] or page eq g.page
        return g
    g.page = page
    g

--- The grid on its next page, when there is one.
fn grid_next[g]
    grid_page_pressed[g, g.page + 1]

--- The grid on the page before, when there is one.
fn grid_previous[g]
    grid_page_pressed[g, g.page - 1]

--- How a grid sorts a column: 'asc', 'desc' or 'neutral'.
fn grid_sort_state[g, index]
    for s in g.sorts
        if s.index eq index
            if s.direction eq 1
                return 'asc'
            return 'desc'
    'neutral'

--- A column's sort button's title: what a press would do (descending once
--- it sorts ascending, else ascending).
fn grid_sort_title[g, lang, index]
    if grid_sort_state[g, index] eq 'asc'
        return lang.sort_descending
    lang.sort_ascending

fn _paged_title[lang, page]
    replace[lang.page_title, '{page}', str[page]]

--- A grid's page buttons as Grid.js lays them out: Previous; a run of
--- `count` page buttons around the page shown (as near the middle as the
--- pages allow), with the first page and a gap before it when the run does
--- not reach the first, and a gap and the last page after it when it does
--- not reach the last; then Next.
fn grid_buttons[g, lang]
    pages = grid_pages[g]
    page = g.page
    out = [GridButton[lang.previous, page eq 0, false, false, lang.previous, page - 1]]
    if g.count gt 0
        most = min[pages, g.count]
        pivot = min[page, most / 2]
        if page + most / 2 gte pages
            pivot = most - (pages - page)
        if pages gt most and page - pivot gt 0
            out = push[out, GridButton['1', false, false, false, lang.first_page, 0]]
            out = push[out, GridButton['...', false, false, true, '', -1]]
        k = 0
        while k lt most
            i = page + (k - pivot)
            out = push[out, GridButton[str[i + 1], false, i eq page, false, _paged_title[lang, i + 1], i]]
            k = k + 1
        if pages gt most and pages gt page + pivot + 1
            out = push[out, GridButton['...', false, false, true, '', -1]]
            out = push[out, GridButton[str[pages], false, false, false, _paged_title[lang, pages], pages - 1]]
    push[out, GridButton[lang.following, pages eq page + 1 or pages eq 0, false, false, lang.following, page + 1]]

--- A grid's summary: "Showing 1 to 10 of 57 results", or '' when its
--- search keeps no rows.
fn grid_summary[g, lang]
    total = grid_total[g]
    if total eq 0
        return ''
    low = g.page * g.limit + 1
    high = min[(g.page + 1) * g.limit, total]
    showing = lang.showing
    to_words = lang.to_words
    of_words = lang.of_words
    results = lang.results
    '`showing` `low` `to_words` `high` `of_words` `total` `results`'

--- A grid's summary's title: "Page 1 of 6".
fn grid_summary_title[g, lang]
    out = replace[lang.navigate, '{page}', str[g.page + 1]]
    replace[out, '{pages}', str[grid_pages[g]]]

--- The text a grid's cell shows.
fn grid_cell_text[v]
    if _blank[v]
        return ''
    shown[v]

-------------------------------------------------------------------- tests

fn _tested_columns[]
    [column['name', 'Name'], column['age', 'Age'], column['team', 'Team']]

fn _tested_rows[]
    [row['r0', map[name = words['Ada'], age = number[36], team = words['red']]], row['r1', map[name = words['bob'], age = number[17], team = words['blue']]], row['r2', map[name = words['Cy'], age = missing[], team = words['red']]], row['r3', map[name = words['dee'], age = number[52], team = words['blue']]], row['r4', map[name = words['Eve'], age = number[17], team = words['red']]]]

fn _tested[]
    new[_tested_columns[], _tested_rows[]]

fn _page_ids[t]
    out = _no_words[]
    for r in models[t].page.rows
        out = push[out, r.id]
    out

fn _tree[]
    leaf = row['a.0.0', map[name = words['zed'], age = number[1], team = words['red']]]
    middle = tree_row['a.0', map[name = words['yan'], age = number[2], team = words['blue']], [leaf]]
    other = row['a.1', map[name = words['xia'], age = number[3], team = words['red']]]
    top = tree_row['a', map[name = words['wes'], age = number[4], team = words['blue']], [middle, other]]
    lone = row['b', map[name = words['vic'], age = number[5], team = words['red']]]
    new[_tested_columns[], [top, lone]]

test numbers_show_as_javascript_writes_them[]
    number_text[0.1 + 0.2] eq '0.30000000000000004'
    number_text[float['1e21']] eq '1e+21'
    number_text[0.00000015] eq '1.5e-7'
    number_text[-0.5] eq '-0.5'
    number_text[123.0] eq '123'
    shown[listed[[number[1], nothing[], words['a']]]] eq '1,,a'

test dates_show_as_javascript_writes_them_in_utc[]
    shown[date[1654041600000.0]] eq 'Wed Jun 01 2022 00:00:00 GMT+0000 (Coordinated Universal Time)'
    shown[date[-1.0]] eq 'Wed Dec 31 1969 23:59:59 GMT+0000 (Coordinated Universal Time)'
    shown[date[0.0 / 0.0]] eq 'Invalid Date'

test alphanumeric_sorting_reads_runs_of_digits_as_numbers[]
    sort_compare['alphanumeric', words['a2'], words['a10']] eq -1
    sort_compare['text', words['a2'], words['a10']] eq 1
    sort_compare['alphanumeric', words['B'], words['a']] eq 1
    sort_compare['alphanumericCaseSensitive', words['B'], words['a']] eq -1

test a_text_column_sorts_up_then_down_then_not_at_all[]
    t = _tested[]
    t = sort_toggled[t, 'name', false]
    _page_ids[t] eq ['r0', 'r1', 'r2', 'r3', 'r4']
    t = sort_toggled[t, 'name', false]
    _page_ids[t] eq ['r4', 'r3', 'r2', 'r1', 'r0']
    t = sort_toggled[t, 'name', false]
    len[t.state.sorting] eq 0

test a_number_column_sorts_down_first_and_counts_a_missing_value_greatest[]
    t = _tested[]
    m = models[t]
    first_sort[t, m, 'age'] eq 'desc'
    t = sort_toggled[t, 'age', false]
    sort_of[t, 'age'] eq 'desc'
    _page_ids[t] eq ['r2', 'r3', 'r0', 'r1', 'r4']
    t = sort_toggled[t, 'age', false]
    _page_ids[t] eq ['r1', 'r4', 'r0', 'r3', 'r2']

test a_shift_press_adds_a_column_to_the_sort[]
    t = _tested[]
    t = sort_toggled[t, 'team', false]
    t = sort_toggled[t, 'age', true]
    len[t.state.sorting] eq 2
    sort_index[t, 'age'] eq 1
    _page_ids[t] eq ['r3', 'r1', 'r2', 'r0', 'r4']
    t = sort_toggled[t, 'name', false]
    len[t.state.sorting] eq 1

test the_sort_keeps_at_most_the_columns_it_may[]
    o = options[most_sorted = 2]
    t = made[_tested_columns[], _tested_rows[], o, empty_state[]]
    t = sort_toggled[t, 'name', true]
    t = sort_toggled[t, 'age', true]
    t = sort_toggled[t, 'team', true]
